window.STUDY_DATA = {"siteTitle":"Psychotherapy Exam Lab","subtitle":"AEDA-aligned interactive revision","modules":[{"id":"foundations","order":1,"title":"Foundations of Psychotherapy & the Helping Profession","short":"Start here","icon":"◉","accent":"indigo","summary":"What psychotherapy is, why people seek it, what makes helping effective, and how the therapist’s personhood and professionalism shape care.","objectives":["Define psychotherapy/counseling as a professional helping process.","Recognize common reasons clients seek psychotherapy.","Explain therapist qualities, beginning-therapist challenges and hazards of the helping profession.","Distinguish broad approaches to psychotherapy and the conditions that help therapy work."],"notes":[{"heading":"Psychotherapy as a helping relationship","body":"Psychotherapy is a structured, purposeful professional relationship aimed at reducing distress, improving functioning, changing maladaptive patterns, building coping skills, increasing insight or helping clients live more deliberately. The relationship is collaborative rather than something done to the client.","mnemonic":"HELP: Human relationship, Ethical frame, Learning/change, Purpose."},{"heading":"Why people seek psychotherapy","body":"People may seek help for symptoms, diagnosable disorders, trauma, grief, relationship difficulties, life transitions, stress, identity questions, maladaptive habits, or personal growth. A presenting complaint is not automatically a diagnosis.","mnemonic":"DISTRESS → assess before you label."},{"heading":"What helps therapy work","body":"A safe alliance, empathy, clear goals, informed consent, confidentiality, therapist competence, cultural responsiveness, client participation, realistic expectations and regular review all support effective work. Technique matters, but it is delivered through the therapeutic relationship.","mnemonic":"ALLIANCE before appliance."},{"heading":"The counselor as person and professional","body":"Effective therapists monitor their own values, biases, vulnerabilities and emotional reactions. Genuineness does not mean boundarylessness. Personal warmth must coexist with competence, ethical discipline, supervision and self-awareness.","mnemonic":"SELF: Self-awareness, Ethics, Limits, Feedback/supervision."},{"heading":"Hazards for helpers","body":"Beginning therapists may over-identify, rescue, give premature advice, fear silence, avoid difficult topics, depend on client approval or become overconfident. Burnout, compassion fatigue and countertransference can impair judgment if not recognized.","mnemonic":"PAUSE: Personal reactions, Advice urge, Uncertainty, Supervision, Ethics."}],"concepts":["techniques-person-centered-therapy","process-active-listening","process-open-ended-questioning","process-paraphrasing","process-reflection-of-feeling","process-therapeutic-confrontation","process-self-disclosure"],"sources":["Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; Issues faced by beginning therapists 5","Counseling as a Helping Profession","Introduction to Psychopathology","Still on Code of ethics","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; Issues faced by beginning therapists 9","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; Characteristics of effective therapists 2","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; Issues faced by beginning therapists 1","Reasons why people seek PST 02","Skilled; Unskilled counselors","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques 2","Approaches to PST 3","Approaches to PST 5","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; Characteristics of effective therapists 1","Brief history of PST","intro to PST","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; Issues faced by beginning therapists 6","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; The counselor as a person and a professional","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; Issues faced by beginning therapists 8","Approaches to PST 2","Code of ethics; standards of practice","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques 1","Approaches to PST","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; Issues faced by beginning therapists 2","Things to note in PST","Goals of PST","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; Issues faced by beginning therapists 4","Counseling relationships continues","Approaches to PST 4","Some reasons why people seek PST","Who seeks PST","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; Characteristics of effective therapists","Hazards of the Helping Profession","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; Issues faced by beginning therapists 3","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; Issues faced by beginning therapists 7","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; Issues faced by beginning therapists","What must be considered for pST to work","Types of psychotherapy"]},{"id":"assessment","order":2,"title":"Assessment, Diagnosis, Case Formulation & Treatment Planning","short":"From story to plan","icon":"⌁","accent":"cyan","summary":"How to move from the client’s story to a careful assessment, a working diagnosis/formulation, SMART goals, a treatment plan and progress review.","objectives":["Separate assessment, diagnosis and case formulation.","Use risk assessment and the mental-status mindset before technique selection.","Organize a formulation using predisposing, precipitating, perpetuating and protective factors.","Build and review a collaborative treatment plan."],"notes":[{"heading":"The 4 Ds of abnormality","body":"A common teaching framework considers Deviance, Distress, Dysfunction and Danger. Context matters: unusual behavior is not automatically pathological, and culture changes how behavior is interpreted.","mnemonic":"4D: Deviance, Distress, Dysfunction, Danger."},{"heading":"Assessment before diagnosis","body":"Assessment gathers symptoms, history, context, functioning, strengths, risk, medical/substance factors and the client’s own meaning. Diagnosis classifies a syndrome; it should follow assessment rather than replace it.","mnemonic":"HISTORY + HERE + HAZARD + HELPERS."},{"heading":"Case formulation","body":"A formulation explains how the problem developed and what keeps it going. A practical structure is presenting problem, predisposing factors, precipitating factors, perpetuating factors and protective factors. It guides intervention more directly than a diagnostic label alone.","mnemonic":"5P: Presenting, Predisposing, Precipitating, Perpetuating, Protective."},{"heading":"Treatment planning","body":"Translate the formulation into prioritized problems, goals, measurable objectives, interventions and review criteria. Good plans are individualized and flexible; they are revised when progress is absent, circumstances change or new risks emerge.","mnemonic":"PGOIR: Problems, Goals, Objectives, Interventions, Review."},{"heading":"SMART goals","body":"Goals are easier to evaluate when they are Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant/Realistic and Time-bound. “Feel better” is vague; “take a 15-minute walk on four days this week” is measurable.","mnemonic":"SMART."},{"heading":"Records and progress review","body":"Clinical records should be accurate, timely, relevant and securely stored. Review progress against the agreed goals, not only against the therapist’s impression of the session.","mnemonic":"ACTS: Accurate, Current, Targeted, Secure."}],"concepts":["process-assessment","process-goal-setting","process-treatment-plan","process-case-formulation","process-progress-review","process-termination-and-follow-up"],"sources":["Treatment plan formulation consideration","Assessment and diagnosis 6","Assessment and diagnosis 5","Assessment and diagnosis 3","Assessment and diagnosis 1","Record keeping for a client","Assessment and Diagnosis","Assessment and diagnosis 10","Steps to effective treatment plan","Assessment and diagnosis 7","Assessment and diagnosis 9","Treatment plan 1","Treatment plan 2","Treatment plan","Record keeping for a client; case notes","Assessment and diagnosis 8","Assessment and diagnosis 2","Assessment and diagnosis 4","Case formulation"]},{"id":"adler","order":3,"title":"Adlerian Therapy","short":"Purpose & responsibility","icon":"↗","accent":"violet","summary":"A goal-directed, socially embedded view of human behavior emphasizing choice, responsibility, lifestyle and encouragement.","objectives":["Recall the course emphasis on purposeful/goal-directed behavior.","Explain consciousness, social relatedness, choice and responsibility in Adlerian work.","Recognize lifestyle and encouragement as practical treatment themes.","Apply Adlerian thinking to a case vignette."],"notes":[{"heading":"View of human nature","body":"The opening course audio explicitly emphasizes behavior as purposeful and goal-directed, gives consciousness major importance, and highlights social relatedness, choice and responsibility. The client is not treated as a passive victim of hidden forces.","mnemonic":"GSCR: Goal-directed, Social, Conscious, Responsible."},{"heading":"Lifestyle and private logic","body":"Adlerian work examines the person’s characteristic way of interpreting self, others and the world—often called lifestyle/private logic—and asks how it serves current goals. Early recollections and family patterns can help illuminate this style.","mnemonic":"Lifestyle = recurring life strategy."},{"heading":"Inferiority and striving","body":"Feelings of inferiority can motivate growth, but rigid attempts to compensate may create problems. Therapy seeks more useful, socially connected ways of striving.","mnemonic":"Inferiority → striving → direction matters."},{"heading":"Encouragement and reorientation","body":"The therapist builds courage, challenges mistaken assumptions and helps the client choose new behavior consistent with social interest and responsibility. Insight is useful when it leads to reorientation and action.","mnemonic":"Understand → Encourage → Reorient → Act."}],"concepts":["techniques-adlerian-therapy"],"sources":["ALFRED ADLER'S THERAPY 4","ALFRED ADLER'S THERAPY 8","ALFRED ADLER'S THERAPY 3","ALFRED ADLER'S THERAPY 1","ALFRED ADLER'S THERAPY 2","ALFRED ADLER'S THERAPY","ALFRED ADLER'S THERAPY 5","ALFRED ADLER'S THERAPY 7","ALFRED ADLER'S THERAPY 6","ALFRED ADLER'S THERAPY; APPLICATION TO THE CASE OF STAN","ALFRED ADLER'S THERAPY; APPLICATION TO THE CASE OF STAN 1","ALFRED ADLER'S THERAPY; APPLICATION TO THE CASE OF STAN 2"]},{"id":"psychoanalytic","order":4,"title":"Psychoanalytic Therapy","short":"Unconscious patterns","icon":"◌","accent":"rose","summary":"Freud, defenses, free association, interpretation, resistance, transference/countertransference and the therapeutic relationship.","objectives":["Differentiate free association, interpretation and resistance.","Distinguish transference from countertransference.","Review ego defenses and the structural model.","Understand why the therapeutic relationship itself becomes clinical material."],"notes":[{"heading":"Structural model","body":"Classical psychoanalytic theory describes id, ego and superego. The id seeks instinctual gratification; the ego mediates reality; the superego represents internalized standards and prohibitions. Conflict can generate anxiety and defensive responses.","mnemonic":"IES: Id = impulse, Ego = executive, Superego = standards."},{"heading":"Defense mechanisms","body":"Defenses reduce psychological conflict or anxiety, usually outside awareness. Examples include repression, denial, projection, displacement, rationalization, regression and sublimation. Defenses are not simply “bad”; their rigidity and cost matter.","mnemonic":"Defense = anxiety management, often unconscious."},{"heading":"Free association & interpretation","body":"In free association, the client says what comes to mind with minimal censorship. Interpretation offers possible meaning in patterns, dreams, defenses, resistance or relationships; timing and readiness matter.","mnemonic":"FIRT: Free association, Interpretation, Resistance, Transference."},{"heading":"Resistance","body":"Resistance is the defensive blocking of threatening material or therapeutic work. It may appear as avoidance, forgetting, lateness, intellectualization or shifts away from emotionally charged themes.","mnemonic":"Resistance = psychological brakes."},{"heading":"Transference & countertransference","body":"Transference is the client’s re-enactment of old relationship expectations with the therapist. Countertransference is the therapist’s emotional response, especially when shaped by the therapist’s own history. Both require reflection; acting them out is not treatment.","mnemonic":"TRANS = client; COUNTER = counselor side."}],"concepts":["techniques-psychoanalytic-therapy","techniques-free-association","techniques-interpretation","techniques-resistance","techniques-transference","techniques-countertransference"],"sources":["Psychoanalytic therapy;  APPLICATION TO THE CASE OF STAN","Psychoanalytic therapy;  Interpretation; Resistance 1","Psychoanalytic therapy; Ego defense mechanisms","Psychoanalytic therapy;  Application to group counseling ","Psychoanalytic therapy; Freud's stages of development","Psychoanalytic therapy; Relationship between the client and the therapist 1","Psychoanalytic therapy; Relationship between the client and the therapist","Psychoanalytic therapy; Counter transference","Psychoanalytic therapy;  APPLICATION TO THE CASE OF STAN 1","Psychoanalytic therapy;  Interpretation; Resistance","Psychoanalytic therapy; Functions of the therapist","Psychoanalytic therapy; Superego","Psychoanalytic therapy;  Analysis and interpretation of transference","Psychoanalytic therapy; Characteristics","Psychoanalytic therapy;  Interpretation technique","Introduction to Psychoanalytic therapy ","Psychoanalytic therapy;  Application to group counseling 1","Psychoanalytic therapy; Role of the therapist"]},{"id":"existential","order":5,"title":"Existential, Person-Centered & Gestalt Approaches","short":"Meaning & awareness","icon":"◇","accent":"amber","summary":"Meaning, choice, authenticity, empathy, unconditional positive regard, congruence, here-and-now awareness and Gestalt experiments.","objectives":["State core existential goals.","Recall Rogers’ core person-centered conditions.","Recognize Gestalt here-and-now techniques.","Differentiate empathic exploration from advice-giving and interpretation."],"notes":[{"heading":"Existential therapy","body":"Existential work focuses on freedom, responsibility, meaning, choice, authenticity, isolation and mortality. Anxiety is not always something to eliminate; it can signal the reality of choice and responsibility.","mnemonic":"FMRC: Freedom, Meaning, Responsibility, Choice."},{"heading":"Person-centered conditions","body":"Rogers emphasized accurate empathy, unconditional positive regard and congruence/genuineness. The therapist creates a climate in which the client can explore experience rather than being directed toward the therapist’s preferred solution.","mnemonic":"EUC: Empathy, Unconditional positive regard, Congruence."},{"heading":"Gestalt here-and-now","body":"Gestalt therapy emphasizes present awareness: what the client is experiencing now in body, emotion, thought and relationship. The therapist may use experiments to make unfinished material vivid.","mnemonic":"NOW = Notice What Occurs."},{"heading":"Empty chair & dream work","body":"The empty-chair technique externalizes an unfinished dialogue. In Gestalt dream work, the client may relive the dream and speak as different parts rather than receiving a fixed symbolic interpretation from the therapist.","mnemonic":"Empty chair = absent dialogue made present."},{"heading":"Retroflection","body":"Retroflection means turning toward oneself what one would like to do to another, or doing to oneself what one wishes another would do.","mnemonic":"RETRO = turn it back on self."}],"concepts":["techniques-existential-therapy","techniques-person-centered-therapy","techniques-gestalt-therapy","techniques-empty-chair-technique","techniques-gestalt-dream-work","techniques-retroflection"],"sources":["EXISTENTIAL THERAPY AND TECHNIQUES 3","EXISTENTIAL THERAPY AND TECHNIQUES 6","EXISTENTIAL THERAPY AND TECHNIQUES 4","EXISTENTIAL THERAPY AND TECHNIQUES 5","EXISTENTIAL THERAPY AND TECHNIQUES ","EXISTENTIAL THERAPY AND TECHNIQUES 1","EXISTENTIAL THERAPY AND TECHNIQUES 7","EXISTENTIAL THERAPY AND TECHNIQUES 2"]},{"id":"cognitive","order":6,"title":"Behavioral, CBT, REBT, DBT & Skills Techniques","short":"Change loops","icon":"⚙","accent":"teal","summary":"Learning principles, reinforcement, exposure, systematic desensitization, cognitive restructuring, REBT, DBT and skills training.","objectives":["Differentiate classical and operant conditioning.","Avoid confusing punishment with negative reinforcement.","Select graded exposure, flooding or systematic desensitization appropriately.","Use the CBT thought-feeling-behavior model and REBT ABC framework."],"notes":[{"heading":"Classical vs operant conditioning","body":"Classical conditioning concerns associations between stimuli; operant conditioning concerns consequences that increase or decrease behavior. Reinforcement increases behavior; punishment decreases behavior. “Positive/negative” means adding/removing, not good/bad.","mnemonic":"Reinforcement ↑ behavior; Punishment ↓ behavior."},{"heading":"Negative reinforcement","body":"A behavior becomes more likely because it removes an aversive stimulus. Fastening a seat belt to stop a warning tone is the classic exam-style example.","mnemonic":"Negative = remove; reinforcement = increase."},{"heading":"Exposure family","body":"Exposure reduces fear through repeated contact with feared cues without the expected catastrophe. In vivo means real-life exposure. Systematic desensitization uses relaxation plus a graded hierarchy. Flooding starts with high-intensity feared material.","mnemonic":"RHE: Relax/coping, Hierarchy, Exposure."},{"heading":"CBT","body":"CBT links situations, thoughts/beliefs, emotions, physiology and behavior. Cognitive restructuring identifies distorted or unhelpful thoughts, examines evidence and develops more balanced alternatives; behavioral experiments test predictions.","mnemonic":"Catch → Check → Change."},{"heading":"REBT","body":"REBT, associated with Albert Ellis, uses the ABC model: Activating event, Belief, Consequence. Therapy disputes irrational beliefs and builds more effective alternatives.","mnemonic":"ABC(D/E): Activating event, Belief, Consequence, Dispute, Effective belief."},{"heading":"DBT & skills training","body":"DBT is especially associated with emotion dysregulation and borderline personality disorder, combining acceptance and change through mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness. Social-skills training uses modeling, rehearsal, feedback and real-life practice.","mnemonic":"DBT: Mindfulness + Distress tolerance + Emotion regulation + Interpersonal effectiveness."}],"concepts":["techniques-classical-conditioning","techniques-operant-conditioning","techniques-negative-reinforcement","techniques-extinction","techniques-social-modeling","techniques-exposure-therapy","techniques-in-vivo-exposure","techniques-systematic-desensitization","techniques-flooding","techniques-progressive-muscle-relaxation","techniques-cognitive-behavioral-therapy-cbt","techniques-cognitive-restructuring","techniques-rational-emotive-behavior-therapy-rebt","techniques-rational-emotive-imagery","techniques-dialectical-behavior-therapy-dbt","techniques-social-skills-training"],"sources":["Official 2025 paper emphasis: CBT, REBT, behavior therapy, exposure, DBT and social-skills training"]},{"id":"anxiety","order":7,"title":"Anxiety, OCD & Trauma-Related Disorders","short":"Fear, worry, avoidance","icon":"≈","accent":"blue","summary":"Panic, GAD, phobias, social anxiety, agoraphobia, OCD and PTSD—with the diagnostic cues and treatment logic examiners repeatedly test.","objectives":["Differentiate a panic attack from panic disorder.","Distinguish generalized worry from circumscribed fear.","Separate obsessions from compulsions.","Recognize PTSD symptom clusters and exposure-based treatment principles."],"notes":[{"heading":"Panic attack vs panic disorder","body":"A panic attack is an episode of intense fear/discomfort with abrupt physical and cognitive symptoms. Panic disorder requires recurrent unexpected attacks plus persistent concern or maladaptive behavior change. A panic attack can occur in other disorders.","mnemonic":"PANIC disorder = Panic Attacks + New worry/avoidance Is Continuing."},{"heading":"GAD and phobias","body":"GAD is broad, excessive and difficult-to-control worry across several domains. Specific phobia is tied to a particular object/situation; social anxiety centers on scrutiny; agoraphobia centers on situations where escape/help may feel difficult.","mnemonic":"GAD = General Anxiety across Domains."},{"heading":"OCD","body":"Obsessions are intrusive thoughts, urges or images; compulsions are repetitive behaviors or mental acts intended to reduce anxiety or prevent feared outcomes. Exposure and response prevention targets the obsession-compulsion cycle.","mnemonic":"O = thought; C = Conduct."},{"heading":"PTSD","body":"After trauma, PTSD involves intrusion, avoidance, negative changes in cognition/mood, and altered arousal/reactivity that persist and impair functioning. Trauma can also increase risk of depression and substance problems.","mnemonic":"IANA: Intrusion, Avoidance, Negative changes, Arousal."}],"concepts":["psychopath-panic-disorder","psychopath-generalized-anxiety-disorder-gad","psychopath-specific-phobia","psychopath-social-anxiety-disorder","psychopath-agoraphobia","psychopath-obsessive-compulsive-disorder-ocd","psychopath-post-traumatic-stress-disorder-ptsd","techniques-exposure-therapy","techniques-systematic-desensitization","techniques-progressive-muscle-relaxation"],"sources":["Anxiety disorder 7","Anxiety disorder 3","Anxiety disorder 4","Anxiety disorder","Anxiety disorder 2","Anxiety disorder 6","Anxiety disorder 1","Other types of anxiety disorder","Anxiety disorder 5"]},{"id":"mood","order":8,"title":"Depression, Mania & Bipolar Disorder","short":"Mood syndromes","icon":"☾","accent":"pink","summary":"Depressive and manic presentations, bipolar patterns, cognitive treatment of depression and safety priorities.","objectives":["Recognize a major depressive syndrome and anhedonia.","Recall common manic features.","Differentiate a mood episode from a chronic personality pattern.","Put suicide-risk assessment before routine technique selection."],"notes":[{"heading":"Major depression","body":"Major depression is not ordinary sadness. A clinically significant syndrome typically includes depressed mood and/or loss of interest plus changes in sleep, interest, guilt/worth, energy, concentration, appetite, psychomotor activity and thoughts of death/suicide, with impairment.","mnemonic":"SIGECAPS."},{"heading":"Mania","body":"Mania involves a distinct period of abnormally elevated/irritable mood and increased energy with features such as decreased need for sleep, grandiosity, pressured speech, racing thoughts/flight of ideas, distractibility, increased activity and risky behavior.","mnemonic":"DIG FAST."},{"heading":"Bipolar disorder","body":"Bipolar disorders involve pathologic mood elevation (mania or hypomania depending on type) and often depressive episodes. Rapid speech and reduced need for sleep in a high-energy state should not be mislabeled as simple anxiety.","mnemonic":"Bipolar = poles of mood, not momentary mood swings."},{"heading":"CBT for depression","body":"Use collaborative formulation, identify negative automatic thoughts, examine evidence, develop balanced alternatives and rebuild activity through graded behavioral activation. Address sleep, isolation and problem-solving while monitoring risk.","mnemonic":"TEA: Thoughts, Emotions, Actions."},{"heading":"Suicide safety","body":"When hopelessness or suicidal thinking appears, ask directly about ideation, plan, intent, means, past behavior and protective factors. Acute risk may require urgent psychiatric/emergency action rather than ordinary outpatient technique.","mnemonic":"Safety before strategy."}],"concepts":["psychopath-major-depressive-disorder","psychopath-manic-episode","psychopath-bipolar-disorder","techniques-cognitive-behavioral-therapy-cbt","techniques-cognitive-restructuring"],"sources":["Depression 3","Depression 9","Depression 1","Depression 4","Depression 2","Depression begins","Depression 6","Depression 5","Depression 7","Depression 8"]},{"id":"psychosis","order":9,"title":"Psychosis & Schizophrenia","short":"Reality testing","icon":"✦","accent":"orange","summary":"Core psychotic symptoms, schizophrenia, brief psychotic disorder and the need to combine psychotherapy with safety and psychiatric care.","objectives":["Identify hallucinations, delusions and disorganized speech.","Recognize negative symptoms/social withdrawal.","Use duration/course to distinguish brief psychosis from schizophrenia-spectrum conditions.","Know when stabilization and psychiatric care take priority."],"notes":[{"heading":"Psychotic symptoms","body":"Psychosis can involve hallucinations, delusions, disorganized thinking/speech, markedly disorganized behavior and impaired reality testing. Schizophrenia also commonly includes negative symptoms such as reduced motivation, emotional expression or social engagement.","mnemonic":"HDDN: Hallucinations, Delusions, Disorganization, Negative symptoms."},{"heading":"Schizophrenia","body":"Diagnosis is clinical and depends on symptom pattern, duration, impairment and exclusion of substances/medical causes. There is no single laboratory test that confirms schizophrenia.","mnemonic":"Clinical course, not one lab test."},{"heading":"Brief psychotic disorder","body":"A sudden psychotic episode with short duration and return toward prior functioning differs from schizophrenia chiefly by course/duration; exam vignettes often hinge on this clue.","mnemonic":"BRIEF = short course matters."},{"heading":"Therapeutic priorities","body":"During acute psychosis, prioritize safety, orientation, psychiatric/medical assessment, medication adherence where prescribed, supportive communication and reduction of overwhelming stimulation. Intensive insight work may be inappropriate until the client is stable.","mnemonic":"STABLE before subtle."}],"concepts":["psychopath-schizophrenia","psychopath-brief-psychotic-disorder"],"sources":["Psychosis 1","Psychosis Begins","Psychosis 2"]},{"id":"eating-substance","order":10,"title":"Eating, Substance, Personality & Neurodevelopmental Presentations","short":"High-yield differentials","icon":"△","accent":"lime","summary":"Eating-disorder patterns, substance-use/intoxication clues, common personality disorders and autism-spectrum cues used in exam vignettes.","objectives":["Differentiate anorexia, bulimia and binge-eating patterns.","Recognize opioid vs stimulant intoxication cues.","Identify borderline, OCPD, schizoid and dependent personality patterns.","Recognize core autism-spectrum social/repetitive features."],"notes":[{"heading":"Eating disorders","body":"Anorexia involves significantly low weight with restriction and fear/behavior preventing weight gain; binge-eating/purging can occur within anorexia when weight remains significantly low. Bulimia involves recurrent binges with compensatory behaviors without the persistently low-weight requirement. Binge-eating disorder lacks regular compensatory behaviors.","mnemonic":"LOW weight → anorexia; binge + compensate → bulimia; binge without regular compensate → BED."},{"heading":"Substance use disorder","body":"Substance use disorder is a problematic pattern causing clinically significant impairment/distress, reflected in impaired control, social impairment, risky use and pharmacologic features such as tolerance/withdrawal. Early intervention can reduce harm and chronicity.","mnemonic":"Control, Consequences, Risk, Body adaptation."},{"heading":"Intoxication clues","body":"Opioids often produce euphoria followed by drowsiness/relaxation and respiratory/pupillary signs; stimulants increase energy, confidence and arousal. Exam questions may use a symptom cluster rather than naming the drug.","mnemonic":"Opioid = down; stimulant = up."},{"heading":"Personality patterns","body":"Borderline personality disorder features instability, abandonment sensitivity, impulsivity and self-harm. OCPD emphasizes rigid perfectionism/control. Schizoid personality emphasizes detachment and limited desire for close relationships. Dependent personality emphasizes excessive reliance on others for decisions/support.","mnemonic":"Borderline = unstable; OCPD = over-controlled; Schizoid = detached; Dependent = needs others."},{"heading":"Autism spectrum disorder","body":"Core features include persistent social-communication difficulties plus restricted/repetitive behaviors or interests, beginning in development. Repetitive movements and unusual language with reduced reciprocal social interaction are classic vignette cues.","mnemonic":"Social communication + Restricted/repetitive."}],"concepts":["psychopath-anorexia-nervosa-restricting-type","psychopath-anorexia-nervosa-binge-eating-purging-type","psychopath-bulimia-nervosa","psychopath-binge-eating-disorder","psychopath-substance-use-disorder","psychopath-opioid-intoxication","psychopath-stimulant-intoxication","psychopath-borderline-personality-disorder","psychopath-obsessive-compulsive-personality-disorder-ocpd","psychopath-schizoid-personality-disorder","psychopath-dependent-personality-disorder","psychopath-autism-spectrum-disorder"],"sources":["Substance use disorder 1","Eating disorders begin","Substance Use disorder begins","Substance use disorder 2","eating disorders 1","Eating disorder 3","Eating disorder 4","Eating disorders 2"]},{"id":"ethics","order":11,"title":"Ethics, Law & Professional Practice","short":"Protect the client","icon":"§","accent":"red","summary":"Ethical principles, consent, confidentiality, duty to warn, mandated reporting, boundaries, malpractice, records, subpoenas, technology and termination.","objectives":["Apply autonomy, beneficence, nonmaleficence, justice, veracity and fidelity.","Know major limits to confidentiality.","Distinguish boundary crossings from violations.","Respond ethically to records, subpoenas, technology and termination."],"notes":[{"heading":"Core ethical principles","body":"Autonomy respects informed choice; beneficence promotes welfare; nonmaleficence avoids harm; justice requires fairness; veracity requires truthfulness; fidelity concerns trust and keeping professional commitments.","mnemonic":"ABNJ-VF: Autonomy, Beneficence, Nonmaleficence, Justice, Veracity, Fidelity."},{"heading":"Informed consent","body":"Clients should understand the nature/purpose of services, risks, benefits, alternatives, fees, therapist role/qualifications, limits of confidentiality and their freedom to ask questions or decline. Consent is a process, not a one-time signature.","mnemonic":"BRALC: Benefits, Risks, Alternatives, Limits, Choice."},{"heading":"Confidentiality & exceptions","body":"Confidentiality is a professional duty, privacy is the client’s right to control access to self/information, and privilege is a legal evidentiary protection. Serious risk, mandated abuse reporting and valid legal requirements can create exceptions that must be handled narrowly and carefully.","mnemonic":"Privacy = person; Confidentiality = clinician; Privilege = proceeding."},{"heading":"Duty to warn/protect & mandated reporting","body":"A serious credible threat to an identifiable person can trigger protective duties depending on applicable law. Mandated reporting requires reports in legally specified situations such as suspected abuse. These are not excuses for casual disclosure.","mnemonic":"TIP: Threat, Identifiable person, Protect."},{"heading":"Boundaries & dual relationships","body":"A boundary crossing may be benign or clinically justified; a boundary violation is harmful/exploitative. Dual relationships can impair objectivity or create power conflicts. Sexual relationships with current clients are unethical.","mnemonic":"Crossing ≠ automatically violation; harm/exploitation marks violation."},{"heading":"Malpractice & competence","body":"Malpractice is professional negligence: duty, breach, causation and harm/damages. Working outside competence can create risk; supervision, referral and continuing education are protective responsibilities.","mnemonic":"DBHC: Duty, Breach, Harm caused (Causation)."},{"heading":"Records, subpoenas & technology","body":"Keep secure, relevant and timely records. A subpoena should not trigger reflex release of the entire file; seek appropriate legal/professional guidance and disclose only what is authorized/required. Teletherapy adds privacy, identity, competence, emergency-location and jurisdiction concerns.","mnemonic":"STOP before disclosure."},{"heading":"Termination","body":"Terminate when goals are met, treatment is no longer beneficial, competence is lacking or another legitimate reason applies—but plan continuity, referrals and reasonable notice to avoid abandonment.","mnemonic":"PLAN: Prepare, Link/referral, Agree ending, Note/document."}],"concepts":["ethics-autonomy","ethics-beneficence","ethics-nonmaleficence","ethics-justice","ethics-veracity","ethics-fidelity","ethics-informed-consent","ethics-confidentiality","ethics-privacy","ethics-privileged-communication","ethics-duty-to-warn-protect","ethics-mandated-reporting","ethics-malpractice","ethics-professional-competence","ethics-dual-or-multiple-relationship","ethics-boundary-crossing","ethics-boundary-violation","ethics-sexual-relationship-with-a-current-client","ethics-gift-decision-making","ethics-bartering","ethics-record-keeping","ethics-termination-and-referral","ethics-ethical-response-to-colleague-misconduct","ethics-technology-ethics","ethics-subpoena-response"],"sources":["Ethical and professional issues begins","More on Informed Consent","Technology and ethical issues","Continuing of education; Dual relationships","Informed consent; termination of therapy","Ethical principle of nonmaleficience 1","Legal concerns in counseling; expert witness","Ethical principle of Autonomy","Ethical principle of Autonomy 1","Professional negligence; Malpractice","Ethical principle of Beneficence; Justice","Competence; progress evaluation; professional conduct","Confidentiality; Dual relationships","Disability concerns; slandering; defamation as ethical concerns","Counseling relationship; responsibilities as per code of ethics","Duty to warn","Ethical principle of nonmaleficence begins","Ethical principle of Veracity"]},{"id":"culture-process","order":12,"title":"Cultural Competence, Group Therapy & Counseling Process","short":"Work with context","icon":"◎","accent":"green","summary":"Cultural humility/competence, interpreter use, group types/stages, core counseling skills, goal setting, termination and follow-up.","objectives":["Avoid ethnocentrism and cultural imposition.","Select group type and recognize group-development stages.","Use core listening/responding skills accurately.","Carry counseling from rapport through termination and follow-up."],"notes":[{"heading":"Cultural competence","body":"A practical framework emphasizes awareness of one’s own assumptions, knowledge of cultural contexts, adaptable clinical skills and genuine encounters with diverse clients. Competence is ongoing; cultural humility prevents the therapist from assuming expertise over the client’s lived experience.","mnemonic":"AKSE: Awareness, Knowledge, Skills, Encounters."},{"heading":"Ethnocentrism & cultural imposition","body":"Ethnocentrism treats one’s own culture as the norm for judging others. Cultural imposition goes further by pressuring clients to adopt the helper’s or dominant culture’s beliefs/practices.","mnemonic":"Centre vs impose."},{"heading":"Interpreter use","body":"When language barriers matter, qualified interpreters support accuracy, confidentiality and informed consent. Children or untrained friends should not routinely carry the burden of sensitive clinical interpretation.","mnemonic":"Interpreter = accuracy + confidentiality."},{"heading":"Group types","body":"Support groups emphasize mutual aid; psychoeducational groups teach information; skills groups practice specific behaviors; CBT groups combine cognitive/behavioral learning with structured exercises.","mnemonic":"Support = share; Psychoeducation = know; Skills = do."},{"heading":"Group development","body":"A common sequence is Forming, Storming, Norming, Performing and Adjourning. Not every group follows it perfectly, and culture/context can alter the pattern.","mnemonic":"FSNPA."},{"heading":"Counseling process","body":"A useful sequence is rapport/engagement, assessment, goals, intervention, review, and termination/follow-up. The stages overlap; safety concerns can interrupt the sequence at any point.","mnemonic":"RAGIRT: Rapport, Assessment, Goals, Intervention, Review, Termination."},{"heading":"Micro-skills","body":"Active listening attends to content, feeling and meaning. Paraphrase restates meaning; reflection names feeling; open questions invite elaboration; summarizing integrates; confrontation respectfully highlights discrepancies. Self-disclosure should serve the client, not the therapist’s need to share.","mnemonic":"LEAP: Listen, Empathize, Ask, Paraphrase."}],"concepts":["process-cultural-competence","process-ethnocentrism","process-cultural-imposition","process-professional-interpreter-use","process-support-group","process-psychoeducational-group","process-skills-development-group","process-cognitive-behavioral-group","process-group-role","process-group-confidentiality","process-forming-stage","process-storming-stage","process-norming-stage","process-performing-stage","process-adjourning-stage","process-rapport-building","process-assessment","process-goal-setting","process-intervention-stage","process-termination-and-follow-up","process-active-listening","process-reflection-of-feeling","process-paraphrasing","process-open-ended-questioning","process-therapeutic-confrontation","process-self-disclosure","process-treatment-plan","process-case-formulation","process-progress-review"],"sources":["Cultural identity 1","Counseling Skills 1","Counseling skills 2","Counseling skills 5","Counseling skills 4","Counseling Skills","Cultural identity","Counseling skills 6","Cultural identity 2","Counseling skills 3"]},{"id":"simulation","order":13,"title":"Clinical Simulation & Exam Technique","short":"Turn knowledge into marks","icon":"✓","accent":"slate","summary":"A practical framework for 25-mark simulation cases: first sessions, phobias, depression, trauma, ethics, cultural cases and treatment planning.","objectives":["Structure a first counseling session.","Name an anchor theory/technique and justify it from the vignette.","Put risk assessment and ethics where they belong.","Write answers as a sequence of clinical actions rather than a list of definitions."],"notes":[{"heading":"The 25-mark answer shape","body":"Start with the presenting problem and immediate safety/ethical priorities. Name the anchor approach. Then show rapport, assessment/formulation, goals, specific techniques, homework/practice, review and follow-up. Refer back to details in the case.","mnemonic":"RAPPORT: Rapport, Agreement/consent, Problem assessment, Prioritize risk, Objectives, Response/technique, Take-home/follow-up."},{"heading":"Diagnosis is not enough","body":"When asked to “carry out a session,” do not spend the whole answer defining the disorder. Examiners can award marks for what you actually say/do: questions, reflections, rationale, technique steps, homework and review.","mnemonic":"SHOW the session, don’t only NAME the theory."},{"heading":"Case-specific technique","body":"Driving phobia calls for graded exposure/systematic desensitization; depression often calls for CBT/behavioral activation plus safety assessment; existential conflict calls for meaning/choice work; ethical crises require protective action before routine therapy.","mnemonic":"Cue → formulation → fitting technique."},{"heading":"Use mini-scripts","body":"Brief examples of therapist language demonstrate skill: “What happens just before the panic peaks?” “When you say ‘I am useless,’ what evidence supports and contradicts that thought?” “Before we continue, I need to check your safety.”","mnemonic":"Question + reflection + rationale."}],"concepts":[],"sources":["Simulation; THE CASE OF STAN 4","Simulation; THE CASE OF STAN 1","Simulation","Simulation; THE CASE OF STAN 5","Simulation; THE CASE OF STAN 3","Simulation; THE CASE OF STAN","Simulation; THE CASE OF STAN 2"]}],"concepts":[{"label":"Panic disorder","cue":"recurrent unexpected panic attacks followed by persistent worry about more attacks or maladaptive avoidance","explanation":"Panic disorder is more than a single panic attack: recurrent unexpected attacks are followed by ongoing concern or behavior change.","mnemonic":"PANIC: Panic Attacks + New worry/avoidance Is Continuing.","id":"psychopath-panic-disorder","category":"psychopath"},{"label":"Generalized anxiety disorder (GAD)","cue":"excessive, difficult-to-control worry across several life areas for about six months, with tension, restlessness or poor sleep","explanation":"GAD is characterized by persistent generalized worry across multiple domains rather than fear tied to one object or situation.","mnemonic":"GAD = General Anxiety across Domains.","id":"psychopath-generalized-anxiety-disorder-gad","category":"psychopath"},{"label":"Specific phobia","cue":"marked fear of a specific object or situation that triggers immediate anxiety and avoidance out of proportion to the actual danger","explanation":"A specific phobia is circumscribed: the fear is linked to a particular object or situation and typically produces avoidance.","mnemonic":"SPECIFIC = one SPECIFIC trigger.","id":"psychopath-specific-phobia","category":"psychopath"},{"label":"Social anxiety disorder","cue":"marked fear of social or performance situations because of possible scrutiny, embarrassment or negative evaluation","explanation":"Social anxiety centers on being observed, judged or embarrassed in social/performance settings.","mnemonic":"SOCIAL = Scrutiny Of Client In Activities with Lots of people.","id":"psychopath-social-anxiety-disorder","category":"psychopath"},{"label":"Agoraphobia","cue":"fear and avoidance of situations where escape may be difficult or help unavailable, such as crowds, public transport or being outside alone","explanation":"Agoraphobia concerns situations in which escape or access to help feels difficult; it may occur with or without panic disorder.","mnemonic":"AGORA = Afraid to Go Out/Risk being Away from help.","id":"psychopath-agoraphobia","category":"psychopath"},{"label":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD)","cue":"intrusive unwanted thoughts or urges and/or repetitive behaviors or mental acts performed to reduce anxiety","explanation":"OCD involves obsessions (intrusive thoughts/urges/images) and compulsions (repetitive acts or mental rituals).","mnemonic":"O = Obsession is a thought; C = Compulsion is a Conduct.","id":"psychopath-obsessive-compulsive-disorder-ocd","category":"psychopath"},{"label":"Post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD)","cue":"after trauma, recurrent intrusions or nightmares, avoidance, negative changes in mood/cognition and heightened arousal persisting beyond one month","explanation":"PTSD follows exposure to trauma and is organized around intrusion, avoidance, negative cognitive/mood changes and arousal/reactivity.","mnemonic":"IANA: Intrusion, Avoidance, Negative changes, Arousal.","id":"psychopath-post-traumatic-stress-disorder-ptsd","category":"psychopath"},{"label":"Major depressive disorder","cue":"at least two weeks of depressed mood and/or loss of interest with associated sleep, appetite, energy, concentration, guilt, psychomotor or suicidal symptoms","explanation":"Major depression is a sustained depressive syndrome, not simply transient sadness. Functional impairment and risk, especially suicidality, must be assessed.","mnemonic":"SIGECAPS: Sleep, Interest, Guilt, Energy, Concentration, Appetite, Psychomotor, Suicide.","id":"psychopath-major-depressive-disorder","category":"psychopath"},{"label":"Manic episode","cue":"abnormally elevated or irritable mood with increased energy, reduced need for sleep, pressured speech, grandiosity, racing thoughts and risky activity","explanation":"A manic episode combines abnormal mood elevation/irritability with increased energy and characteristic activation symptoms.","mnemonic":"DIG FAST: Distractible, Indiscretion, Grandiosity, Flight of ideas, Activity, Sleep less, Talkative.","id":"psychopath-manic-episode","category":"psychopath"},{"label":"Bipolar disorder","cue":"a history of a manic episode, often alternating with periods of depression","explanation":"The defining feature of bipolar I disorder is a manic episode; depressive episodes are common but not required for that diagnosis.","mnemonic":"BI-polar = mood can move toward two poles; mania is the key flag.","id":"psychopath-bipolar-disorder","category":"psychopath"},{"label":"Schizophrenia","cue":"persistent psychotic illness with delusions, hallucinations and/or disorganized speech or behavior, often with social or occupational dysfunction","explanation":"Schizophrenia is a chronic psychotic disorder; diagnosis depends on characteristic symptoms, duration and functional impact, not on a laboratory test alone.","mnemonic":"3D psychosis: Delusions, perceptual Disturbance, Disorganization.","id":"psychopath-schizophrenia","category":"psychopath"},{"label":"Brief psychotic disorder","cue":"sudden onset of delusions, hallucinations or disorganized speech/behavior lasting at least a day but less than a month, with return toward baseline","explanation":"The very short duration and return toward premorbid functioning distinguish brief psychotic disorder from longer psychotic disorders.","mnemonic":"BRIEF = psychosis Below one month.","id":"psychopath-brief-psychotic-disorder","category":"psychopath"},{"label":"Anorexia nervosa, restricting type","cue":"significantly low body weight, intense fear of weight gain and restriction without recurrent binge-purge episodes","explanation":"Anorexia nervosa requires significantly low weight plus fear of gaining weight or persistent behavior interfering with weight gain and body-image disturbance.","mnemonic":"ANOREXIA: restriction + low weight + fear of weight gain.","id":"psychopath-anorexia-nervosa-restricting-type","category":"psychopath"},{"label":"Anorexia nervosa, binge-eating/purging type","cue":"significantly low body weight with anorexia features plus recurrent binge-eating or purging","explanation":"The patient meets anorexia criteria including low weight, but also has recurrent binge/purge behavior.","mnemonic":"Low weight first: if anorexia criteria are met, binge/purge does not automatically mean bulimia.","id":"psychopath-anorexia-nervosa-binge-eating-purging-type","category":"psychopath"},{"label":"Bulimia nervosa","cue":"recurrent binge eating followed by compensatory behaviors such as vomiting, laxatives or excessive exercise, without the persistently low weight required for anorexia","explanation":"Bulimia is defined by binge episodes and inappropriate compensation; weight may be normal or above/below normal but not at the markedly low anorexia threshold.","mnemonic":"BULIMIA = Binge + Undo.","id":"psychopath-bulimia-nervosa","category":"psychopath"},{"label":"Binge-eating disorder","cue":"recurrent binge eating with loss of control and distress but without regular compensatory purging or fasting","explanation":"Binge-eating disorder differs from bulimia because recurrent compensatory behaviors are absent.","mnemonic":"BED = Binge, no regular Erasing behavior.","id":"psychopath-binge-eating-disorder","category":"psychopath"},{"label":"Substance use disorder","cue":"a problematic pattern of substance use causing clinically significant impairment or distress, including loss of control, risky use, social impairment or tolerance/withdrawal","explanation":"Substance use disorder is diagnosed from a maladaptive pattern and consequences, not simply from having used a substance.","mnemonic":"4C idea: Control loss, Consequences, Compulsion/craving, Continued use.","id":"psychopath-substance-use-disorder","category":"psychopath"},{"label":"Opioid intoxication","cue":"initial euphoria followed by drowsiness or apathy, with slowed functioning and often constricted pupils","explanation":"Opioids typically produce analgesia, euphoria followed by sedation and physiologic slowing.","mnemonic":"OPIOID = Often Pinpoint pupils, Inward/sedated, Opioid drowsiness.","id":"psychopath-opioid-intoxication","category":"psychopath"},{"label":"Stimulant intoxication","cue":"euphoria, increased energy and confidence, decreased need for sleep, agitation and autonomic activation","explanation":"Stimulants activate rather than sedate; cocaine and amphetamines commonly produce energy, alertness and agitation.","mnemonic":"STIMULANT = STIMulates.","id":"psychopath-stimulant-intoxication","category":"psychopath"},{"label":"Borderline personality disorder","cue":"pervasive instability in relationships, self-image and affect, impulsivity, abandonment fears and recurrent self-harm or suicidal behavior","explanation":"Borderline personality disorder is marked by instability and intense reactivity across relationships, identity, mood and behavior.","mnemonic":"BORDER: Bonds unstable, Overreactive affect, Risky acts, Desertion fear, Empty/identity shifts, Recurrent self-harm.","id":"psychopath-borderline-personality-disorder","category":"psychopath"},{"label":"Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder (OCPD)","cue":"perfectionism, orderliness, rigidity and control that interfere with flexibility and task completion","explanation":"OCPD is a personality pattern of perfectionism and control; unlike OCD, true obsessions and compulsions are not required.","mnemonic":"OCPD = Order, Control, Perfectionism, Duty.","id":"psychopath-obsessive-compulsive-personality-disorder-ocpd","category":"psychopath"},{"label":"Schizoid personality disorder","cue":"detachment from social relationships and restricted emotional expression with little desire for close relationships","explanation":"The key pattern is social detachment and limited affect, rather than fear of rejection as in avoidant personality.","mnemonic":"SCHIZOID = Solitary by preference.","id":"psychopath-schizoid-personality-disorder","category":"psychopath"},{"label":"Dependent personality disorder","cue":"excessive need to be cared for, difficulty making decisions independently, submissiveness and fear of separation","explanation":"Dependent personality is characterized by reliance on others for decisions, reassurance and support.","mnemonic":"DEPENDENT = Decisions Entrusted to People; separation fear.","id":"psychopath-dependent-personality-disorder","category":"psychopath"},{"label":"Autism spectrum disorder","cue":"persistent social-communication difficulties together with restricted or repetitive patterns of behavior or interests beginning in development","explanation":"Autism requires both social-communication differences and restricted/repetitive behavior patterns.","mnemonic":"2R domains: Relating/communication + Repetitive/restricted behavior.","id":"psychopath-autism-spectrum-disorder","category":"psychopath"},{"label":"Free association","cue":"the client says whatever thoughts, feelings, images or memories come to mind without censoring them","explanation":"Free association is a classic psychoanalytic technique intended to reduce censorship and reveal unconscious material.","mnemonic":"Psychoanalytic FIRT: Free association, Interpretation, Resistance, Transference.","id":"techniques-free-association","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Interpretation","cue":"the therapist explains or offers meaning about patterns, defenses, dreams, resistance or behavior","explanation":"Interpretation is the analyst's attempt to make latent or previously unconscious meaning more understandable to the client.","mnemonic":"Interpretation = therapist offers possible meaning.","id":"techniques-interpretation","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Resistance","cue":"the client unconsciously avoids material or processes that threaten to bring unacceptable conflict into awareness","explanation":"Resistance is the defensive blocking of threatening material or therapeutic work.","mnemonic":"Resistance = psychological brakes.","id":"techniques-resistance","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Transference","cue":"the client redirects feelings, expectations or relationship patterns from important past relationships onto the therapist","explanation":"Transference originates primarily from the client's earlier relationships and becomes enacted in the therapeutic relationship.","mnemonic":"TRANSference = client TRANSfers old relationship feelings to therapist.","id":"techniques-transference","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Countertransference","cue":"the therapist's emotional reactions to a client, especially reactions influenced by the therapist's own history or unresolved issues","explanation":"Countertransference concerns the therapist's responses to the client and must be monitored through self-awareness and supervision.","mnemonic":"COUNTER = counselor side of the relational reaction.","id":"techniques-countertransference","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Psychoanalytic therapy","cue":"therapy emphasizing unconscious conflict, early relationships, defenses, transference, resistance and insight","explanation":"Psychoanalytic approaches seek insight into unconscious patterns and how early relationships repeat in present life and therapy.","mnemonic":"FIRT: Free association, Interpretation, Resistance, Transference.","id":"techniques-psychoanalytic-therapy","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Adlerian therapy","cue":"a goal-directed, socially embedded approach emphasizing conscious choice, responsibility, lifestyle and movement from inferiority toward useful belonging","explanation":"Adlerian therapy views behavior as purposeful and socially related, with emphasis on goals, choice and responsibility. This is explicitly reflected in the opening lecture of the fused audio.","mnemonic":"GSCR: Goal-directed, Social relatedness, Conscious choice, Responsibility.","id":"techniques-adlerian-therapy","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Person-centered therapy","cue":"a non-directive relationship based on accurate empathy, unconditional positive regard and congruence","explanation":"Rogers' person-centered therapy relies on core facilitative conditions rather than therapist-imposed solutions.","mnemonic":"EUC: Empathy, Unconditional positive regard, Congruence.","id":"techniques-person-centered-therapy","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT)","cue":"a structured approach linking thoughts, emotions and behaviors and using cognitive and behavioral change strategies","explanation":"CBT targets maladaptive thinking and behavior through collaborative, structured methods such as cognitive restructuring and behavioral experiments.","mnemonic":"TEA: Thoughts - Emotions - Actions.","id":"techniques-cognitive-behavioral-therapy-cbt","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Cognitive restructuring","cue":"identifying inaccurate or dysfunctional thoughts, examining evidence and developing more balanced alternatives","explanation":"Cognitive restructuring is a core CBT method for testing and modifying distorted or unhelpful cognitions.","mnemonic":"Catch it, Check it, Change it.","id":"techniques-cognitive-restructuring","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy (REBT)","cue":"Albert Ellis' approach that identifies irrational beliefs between events and emotional consequences and actively disputes those beliefs","explanation":"REBT uses the ABC model and disputation to replace rigid irrational beliefs with more rational alternatives.","mnemonic":"ABCDE: Activating event, Belief, Consequence, Dispute, Effective new belief.","id":"techniques-rational-emotive-behavior-therapy-rebt","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Rational emotive imagery","cue":"an REBT imagery exercise in which the client vividly imagines a difficult event, experiences the emotion, then practices changing unhealthy emotion by changing beliefs","explanation":"Rational emotive imagery is an experiential REBT technique for rehearsing healthier emotional responses through belief change.","mnemonic":"Imagine - feel - dispute - re-feel.","id":"techniques-rational-emotive-imagery","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Gestalt therapy","cue":"a here-and-now experiential approach emphasizing awareness, contact, unfinished business and integration","explanation":"Gestalt therapy focuses on present awareness and experiments that help clients own and integrate experience.","mnemonic":"Gestalt = awareness in the NOW.","id":"techniques-gestalt-therapy","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Empty-chair technique","cue":"the client speaks to an imagined person or part of self placed in an empty chair, often switching roles to deepen awareness","explanation":"The empty-chair experiment is associated with Gestalt work on unfinished business, polarities and present-moment experience.","mnemonic":"Empty chair = make the absent relationship present.","id":"techniques-empty-chair-technique","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Gestalt dream work","cue":"the client re-enacts a dream in the present and may speak as different people, objects or elements of the dream","explanation":"Rather than imposing a fixed interpretation, Gestalt dream work invites the client to own and experience parts of the dream.","mnemonic":"Dream it, become it, speak it.","id":"techniques-gestalt-dream-work","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Retroflection","cue":"turning back onto oneself what one wants to do to another, or doing to oneself what one wishes another would do","explanation":"Retroflection is a Gestalt contact-boundary disturbance directed back toward the self.","mnemonic":"RETRO = return action back to self.","id":"techniques-retroflection","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Existential therapy","cue":"an approach emphasizing freedom, responsibility, choice, meaning, authenticity, isolation and awareness of mortality","explanation":"Existential therapy helps clients face givens of existence and make authentic choices rather than simply trying to eliminate all anxiety.","mnemonic":"FMRC: Freedom, Meaning, Responsibility, Choice.","id":"techniques-existential-therapy","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Classical conditioning","cue":"learning by association between stimuli so that a previously neutral cue comes to elicit a response","explanation":"Classical conditioning explains learned associations such as fear conditioning and underlies some exposure-based principles.","mnemonic":"Classical = cue-to-cue association.","id":"techniques-classical-conditioning","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Operant conditioning","cue":"behavior is shaped by its consequences, including reinforcement and punishment","explanation":"Operant conditioning focuses on consequences that increase or decrease behavior.","mnemonic":"Operant = behavior OPERATES, consequence follows.","id":"techniques-operant-conditioning","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Negative reinforcement","cue":"a behavior increases because it removes or prevents an aversive stimulus","explanation":"Negative reinforcement strengthens behavior; negative means something unpleasant is removed, not that behavior is punished.","mnemonic":"Reinforcement always raises behavior; negative removes something.","id":"techniques-negative-reinforcement","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Extinction","cue":"a previously reinforced behavior decreases when the reinforcement maintaining it is consistently withheld","explanation":"Extinction occurs when reinforcement is no longer provided for a behavior.","mnemonic":"Extinction = extinguish the payoff.","id":"techniques-extinction","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Social modeling","cue":"learning behavior by observing what happens to another person or by imitating a model","explanation":"Modeling is observational learning: the client learns from another person's behavior and its consequences.","mnemonic":"MODEL = learn by watching a model.","id":"techniques-social-modeling","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Exposure therapy","cue":"planned contact with feared stimuli, memories or situations while reducing avoidance so fear can be relearned","explanation":"Exposure reduces avoidance and allows corrective learning; it may be in vivo, imaginal or other formats.","mnemonic":"Approach, do not avoid.","id":"techniques-exposure-therapy","category":"techniques"},{"label":"In vivo exposure","cue":"direct, real-life exposure to a feared object or situation","explanation":"In vivo means exposure in the actual situation rather than only in imagination.","mnemonic":"VIVO = in real life.","id":"techniques-in-vivo-exposure","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Systematic desensitization","cue":"gradual exposure to a fear hierarchy combined with relaxation or another incompatible response","explanation":"Systematic desensitization classically combines relaxation training with stepwise movement through a fear hierarchy.","mnemonic":"RHE: Relax, Hierarchy, Expose gradually.","id":"techniques-systematic-desensitization","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Flooding","cue":"rapid and prolonged exposure to a high-intensity feared stimulus or situation without gradual hierarchy progression","explanation":"Flooding differs from graded exposure because the client confronts a high level of fear more rapidly.","mnemonic":"Flooding = jump into the deep end.","id":"techniques-flooding","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Progressive muscle relaxation","cue":"systematically tensing and releasing muscle groups to learn physical relaxation and reduce arousal","explanation":"PMR builds awareness of tension and relaxation by working through muscle groups.","mnemonic":"Tense - notice - release - breathe.","id":"techniques-progressive-muscle-relaxation","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT)","cue":"a skills-based therapy especially associated with emotion dysregulation and borderline personality disorder, using mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness","explanation":"DBT combines acceptance and change strategies and teaches concrete skills for severe emotion dysregulation.","mnemonic":"M-D-E-I: Mindfulness, Distress tolerance, Emotion regulation, Interpersonal effectiveness.","id":"techniques-dialectical-behavior-therapy-dbt","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Social skills training","cue":"behavioral teaching and rehearsal of interpersonal skills such as greetings, eye contact, listening and conversation","explanation":"Social skills training uses instruction, modeling, rehearsal and feedback to build interpersonal behavior.","mnemonic":"IMRF: Instruction, Modeling, Rehearsal, Feedback.","id":"techniques-social-skills-training","category":"techniques"},{"label":"Autonomy","cue":"respecting the client's right to make informed choices about treatment","explanation":"Autonomy supports informed choice, consent and respect for the client's self-determination.","mnemonic":"A = Allow informed choice.","id":"ethics-autonomy","category":"ethics"},{"label":"Beneficence","cue":"acting to promote the client's welfare and benefit","explanation":"Beneficence asks the professional to actively contribute to the client's well-being.","mnemonic":"BENEFIT = beneficence.","id":"ethics-beneficence","category":"ethics"},{"label":"Nonmaleficence","cue":"avoiding or minimizing harm","explanation":"Nonmaleficence is the duty to do no harm and to avoid exposing clients to unnecessary risk.","mnemonic":"NON-maleficence = NO harm.","id":"ethics-nonmaleficence","category":"ethics"},{"label":"Justice","cue":"fair and equitable distribution of services, benefits, burdens and opportunities","explanation":"Justice concerns fairness and equitable treatment, including access and resource allocation.","mnemonic":"Justice = fairness.","id":"ethics-justice","category":"ethics"},{"label":"Veracity","cue":"truthfulness and honest communication with clients","explanation":"Veracity requires accurate, truthful information rather than deception.","mnemonic":"VERACITY = verifiable truth.","id":"ethics-veracity","category":"ethics"},{"label":"Fidelity","cue":"keeping commitments, maintaining trust and being faithful to professional responsibilities","explanation":"Fidelity concerns loyalty, promises and trust within professional relationships.","mnemonic":"Fidelity = faithful to promises.","id":"ethics-fidelity","category":"ethics"},{"label":"Informed consent","cue":"an ongoing process in which the client receives understandable information about services, risks, benefits, alternatives, limits of confidentiality and choice","explanation":"Informed consent is not merely a signature; it is an ongoing communication process supporting voluntary, informed participation.","mnemonic":"BRALC: Benefits, Risks, Alternatives, Limits, Choice.","id":"ethics-informed-consent","category":"ethics"},{"label":"Confidentiality","cue":"the professional duty to protect information learned in the therapeutic relationship, subject to recognized legal and ethical exceptions","explanation":"Confidentiality protects client information but is not absolute; limits should be explained early.","mnemonic":"Confidentiality = keep private, know the exceptions.","id":"ethics-confidentiality","category":"ethics"},{"label":"Privacy","cue":"the client's broader right to control access to self, body and personal information","explanation":"Privacy is the client's right to be left alone or control access, whereas confidentiality is the professional's duty regarding information received.","mnemonic":"Privacy belongs to the person; confidentiality binds the professional.","id":"ethics-privacy","category":"ethics"},{"label":"Privileged communication","cue":"a legal protection that may prevent compelled disclosure of certain therapy communications in judicial proceedings, depending on jurisdiction","explanation":"Privilege is a legal evidentiary concept and is distinct from the broader ethical duty of confidentiality.","mnemonic":"Privilege = courtroom/legal shield.","id":"ethics-privileged-communication","category":"ethics"},{"label":"Duty to warn/protect","cue":"taking reasonable protective action when a client presents a serious, credible threat toward an identifiable person, as required by applicable law and ethics","explanation":"The course tests duty to warn/protect as an exception to confidentiality when serious danger to others is present. Actual legal requirements vary by jurisdiction.","mnemonic":"TIP: Threat serious, Identifiable target, Protect reasonably.","id":"ethics-duty-to-warn-protect","category":"ethics"},{"label":"Mandated reporting","cue":"reporting suspected abuse or other legally reportable situations to designated authorities when law or professional duty requires it","explanation":"Mandated reporting is a recognized limit to confidentiality; therapists should explain this limit during informed consent.","mnemonic":"Report when law makes reporting mandatory.","id":"ethics-mandated-reporting","category":"ethics"},{"label":"Malpractice","cue":"professional negligence in which a duty is breached and the breach causes harm","explanation":"Malpractice requires more than an unfavorable outcome: there must be a professional duty, breach, harm and causal link.","mnemonic":"DBHC: Duty, Breach, Harm, Causation.","id":"ethics-malpractice","category":"ethics"},{"label":"Professional competence","cue":"practicing only within areas supported by appropriate education, training, supervised experience and current skill","explanation":"Using techniques without adequate training can expose clients to harm and the professional to ethical or legal liability.","mnemonic":"Know your scope; seek supervision or refer.","id":"ethics-professional-competence","category":"ethics"},{"label":"Dual or multiple relationship","cue":"a situation in which the therapist has another significant role or relationship with the client in addition to therapy","explanation":"Multiple relationships are not automatically harmful in every context, but should be avoided or carefully managed when they risk exploitation, impaired objectivity or harm.","mnemonic":"Two roles = review power, objectivity, exploitation and harm.","id":"ethics-dual-or-multiple-relationship","category":"ethics"},{"label":"Boundary crossing","cue":"a departure from customary therapeutic boundaries that may be clinically appropriate and non-exploitative in context","explanation":"A boundary crossing is not automatically unethical; context, culture, intent and effect matter.","mnemonic":"Crossing may be thoughtful; violation causes or risks serious harm/exploitation.","id":"ethics-boundary-crossing","category":"ethics"},{"label":"Boundary violation","cue":"a serious harmful or exploitative breach of therapeutic boundaries","explanation":"Boundary violations exploit the therapeutic relationship or cause significant harm and are unethical.","mnemonic":"Violation = harm/exploitation.","id":"ethics-boundary-violation","category":"ethics"},{"label":"Sexual relationship with a current client","cue":"sexual or romantic involvement with a current therapy client","explanation":"Sexual relationships with current clients are prohibited because of power imbalance and exploitation risk. Terminating therapy to start a relationship does not erase those concerns.","mnemonic":"Current client + sex = no.","id":"ethics-sexual-relationship-with-a-current-client","category":"ethics"},{"label":"Gift decision-making","cue":"considering clinical meaning, value, timing, culture, power and exploitation risk before accepting or declining a client gift","explanation":"Gifts require contextual judgment. A culturally meaningful modest gift may be handled differently from an expensive or coercive gift.","mnemonic":"VALUE: Value, Understanding/meaning, Local culture, Unequal power, Effect on therapy.","id":"ethics-gift-decision-making","category":"ethics"},{"label":"Bartering","cue":"accepting goods or services instead of money when ethically permissible, not exploitative and not clinically contraindicated","explanation":"Bartering may be acceptable under some codes when not exploitative and when arrangements are clear and clinically appropriate.","mnemonic":"Barter only if Boundaries and Benefits are safe.","id":"ethics-bartering","category":"ethics"},{"label":"Record keeping","cue":"maintaining accurate, timely, secure and clinically appropriate client records","explanation":"Records support continuity of care, accountability and legal/ethical duties; access must be controlled and retention rules followed.","mnemonic":"ACTS: Accurate, Current, Timely, Secure.","id":"ethics-record-keeping","category":"ethics"},{"label":"Termination and referral","cue":"ending therapy responsibly with preparation, continuity planning and referral when needed rather than abandoning the client","explanation":"Ethical termination includes adequate notice, discussion, referrals or continuity plans when appropriate.","mnemonic":"PLAN: Prepare, Link/referral, Address feelings, Next steps.","id":"ethics-termination-and-referral","category":"ethics"},{"label":"Ethical response to colleague misconduct","cue":"using ethical codes, consultation/supervision and appropriate reporting procedures when credible serious misconduct is observed","explanation":"Serious ethical violations should not simply be ignored; follow code, organizational and legal procedures while protecting clients and due process.","mnemonic":"C-A-R-E: Consult code, Assess facts, Report appropriately, Ensure client safety.","id":"ethics-ethical-response-to-colleague-misconduct","category":"ethics"},{"label":"Technology ethics","cue":"protecting privacy, competence, informed consent, identity, emergency planning and data security when using digital or remote services","explanation":"Technology changes the medium, not the professional duty. Security, consent and contingency planning remain essential.","mnemonic":"SPACE: Security, Privacy, Access/identity, Consent, Emergency plan.","id":"ethics-technology-ethics","category":"ethics"},{"label":"Subpoena response","cue":"not automatically disclosing the entire file on receipt of a subpoena; consult applicable law, the client/attorney and professional guidance, and disclose only as authorized or legally required","explanation":"A subpoena and a court order are not always identical. The safest course-based principle is not to reflexively release all records; seek proper legal/ethical guidance and protect confidentiality to the extent permitted.","mnemonic":"STOP: See legal guidance, Tell/consult client, Only required information, Protect confidentiality.","id":"ethics-subpoena-response","category":"ethics"},{"label":"Cultural competence","cue":"ongoing development of awareness, cultural knowledge, clinical skills and meaningful encounters with diverse clients","explanation":"Cultural competence requires reflective awareness plus knowledge, skills and real encounters; it is not achieved by assuming everyone should conform to the therapist's culture.","mnemonic":"AKSE: Awareness, Knowledge, Skills, Encounters.","id":"process-cultural-competence","category":"process"},{"label":"Ethnocentrism","cue":"judging other cultures primarily by the standards of one's own culture and treating one's own as the norm","explanation":"Ethnocentrism places one's own cultural frame at the center and may distort assessment or communication.","mnemonic":"ETHNO-centred = my culture at the centre.","id":"process-ethnocentrism","category":"process"},{"label":"Cultural imposition","cue":"pressuring a client to adopt the therapist's or dominant culture's beliefs, values or practices","explanation":"Cultural imposition occurs when the helper pushes their own values or practices onto the client.","mnemonic":"IMPOSITION = impose my culture.","id":"process-cultural-imposition","category":"process"},{"label":"Professional interpreter use","cue":"using a qualified interpreter rather than relying on a child or untrained friend when language barriers affect care","explanation":"Qualified interpreters improve accuracy, confidentiality and informed consent.","mnemonic":"Interpreter = accuracy + confidentiality.","id":"process-professional-interpreter-use","category":"process"},{"label":"Support group","cue":"a group emphasizing mutual support, shared experience, acceptance and coping among people facing similar problems","explanation":"Support groups reduce isolation and offer mutual aid through shared experience.","mnemonic":"SUPPORT = Similar people Understanding and Providing Peer help.","id":"process-support-group","category":"process"},{"label":"Psychoeducational group","cue":"a structured group focused on teaching information, understanding a condition and coping knowledge","explanation":"Psychoeducational groups primarily teach information and understanding; they may include discussion and practice.","mnemonic":"EDU = education is central.","id":"process-psychoeducational-group","category":"process"},{"label":"Skills-development group","cue":"a group focused on learning and practicing specific behavioral or interpersonal skills","explanation":"Skills groups emphasize rehearsal, practice and feedback rather than information alone.","mnemonic":"SKILLS = Show, Keep practicing, Improve, Learn, feedback.","id":"process-skills-development-group","category":"process"},{"label":"Cognitive-behavioral group","cue":"a structured group using cognitive and behavioral methods such as thought monitoring, skills practice and behavioral exercises","explanation":"CBT groups combine structured learning with practice and feedback around thoughts and behaviors.","mnemonic":"Group CBT = Think + Do together.","id":"process-cognitive-behavioral-group","category":"process"},{"label":"Group role","cue":"a recurrent functional position adopted by a member, such as scapegoat, monopolizer, peacemaker or spokesperson","explanation":"Group roles describe patterned ways members participate and influence group dynamics.","mnemonic":"Role = repeated position in the group.","id":"process-group-role","category":"process"},{"label":"Group confidentiality","cue":"an agreement that members protect information disclosed by other members, while the leader explains that peer confidentiality cannot be guaranteed in the same way as professional confidentiality","explanation":"Confidentiality is essential in groups, but leaders should explain realistic limits because members are not all licensed professionals.","mnemonic":"What is said in group should stay in group - with limits explained.","id":"process-group-confidentiality","category":"process"},{"label":"Forming stage","cue":"early group stage characterized by orientation, uncertainty, dependence and testing","explanation":"Forming is the beginning stage in which members learn the purpose, structure and safety of the group.","mnemonic":"FSNPA: Forming, Storming, Norming, Performing, Adjourning.","id":"process-forming-stage","category":"process"},{"label":"Storming stage","cue":"group stage in which conflict, resistance, competition or emotional testing becomes more visible","explanation":"Storming often brings disagreement and challenges to roles or leadership.","mnemonic":"Storm = conflict weather.","id":"process-storming-stage","category":"process"},{"label":"Norming stage","cue":"group stage in which cohesion, trust, norms and cooperation consolidate","explanation":"Norming is marked by clearer expectations and stronger cohesion.","mnemonic":"Norming = norms settle.","id":"process-norming-stage","category":"process"},{"label":"Performing stage","cue":"mature working stage in which members use the group productively toward goals","explanation":"Performing is the task-focused, therapeutically productive phase.","mnemonic":"Performing = productive work.","id":"process-performing-stage","category":"process"},{"label":"Adjourning stage","cue":"ending stage involving review, separation, consolidation and transfer of learning","explanation":"Adjourning addresses termination and helps members carry gains beyond the group.","mnemonic":"Adjourning = goodbye and generalize gains.","id":"process-adjourning-stage","category":"process"},{"label":"Rapport building","cue":"creating safety, trust, empathy and collaborative engagement at the beginning of counseling","explanation":"Rapport supports disclosure and collaboration; it begins early but continues throughout treatment.","mnemonic":"RAPPORT starts with Respect, Attention, Presence.","id":"process-rapport-building","category":"process"},{"label":"Assessment","cue":"systematically exploring the client's problems, symptoms, context, history, strengths, risk and functioning","explanation":"Assessment precedes or accompanies formulation and goal setting; it includes both problems and resources.","mnemonic":"HRSF: History, Risk, Symptoms, Functioning.","id":"process-assessment","category":"process"},{"label":"Goal setting","cue":"collaboratively defining client-centered outcomes that are realistic and observable","explanation":"Goals are stronger when the client participates and the goals are specific enough to evaluate.","mnemonic":"SMART: Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Realistic/Relevant, Time-bound.","id":"process-goal-setting","category":"process"},{"label":"Intervention stage","cue":"implementing agreed therapeutic strategies and monitoring the client's response","explanation":"Intervention follows assessment and goal definition and should be linked to the case formulation and treatment plan.","mnemonic":"Assess -> Goal -> Intervene -> Review.","id":"process-intervention-stage","category":"process"},{"label":"Termination and follow-up","cue":"reviewing progress, consolidating coping skills, planning relapse prevention or referral and ending the professional relationship responsibly","explanation":"Termination is a therapeutic phase, not simply stopping appointments.","mnemonic":"RECAP: Review, Evaluate, Consolidate, Aftercare, Part.","id":"process-termination-and-follow-up","category":"process"},{"label":"Active listening","cue":"attending closely to verbal and nonverbal communication and showing the client that the message has been heard and understood","explanation":"Active listening includes presence, minimal encouragers, reflection, clarification and appropriate nonverbal attending.","mnemonic":"SOLER: Squarely face, Open posture, Lean, Eye contact, Relaxed.","id":"process-active-listening","category":"process"},{"label":"Reflection of feeling","cue":"identifying and feeding back the emotional meaning expressed by the client","explanation":"Reflection of feeling helps clients recognize and explore emotions rather than only facts.","mnemonic":"Name the feeling, not just the facts.","id":"process-reflection-of-feeling","category":"process"},{"label":"Paraphrasing","cue":"restating the essence of the client's content in fresh words without changing its meaning","explanation":"Paraphrasing checks understanding and encourages elaboration.","mnemonic":"Paraphrase = same meaning, new words.","id":"process-paraphrasing","category":"process"},{"label":"Open-ended questioning","cue":"questions that invite elaboration rather than a yes/no answer","explanation":"Open questions support exploration, while excessive questioning can turn therapy into an interrogation.","mnemonic":"WHAT/HOW usually open more than DID/IS.","id":"process-open-ended-questioning","category":"process"},{"label":"Therapeutic confrontation","cue":"respectfully pointing out discrepancies between a client's words, behaviors, values or nonverbal cues","explanation":"Confrontation is not aggression; it is a careful invitation to examine inconsistency.","mnemonic":"Confront discrepancy, not the person.","id":"process-therapeutic-confrontation","category":"process"},{"label":"Self-disclosure","cue":"the therapist deliberately shares limited personal information when it is likely to benefit the client rather than meet the therapist's own needs","explanation":"Self-disclosure should be purposeful, brief and client-centered.","mnemonic":"WAIT: Why am I telling this?","id":"process-self-disclosure","category":"process"},{"label":"Treatment plan","cue":"a collaborative written or explicit plan linking identified problems to goals, measurable objectives, interventions and review","explanation":"Treatment plans organize care around the client's specific needs and should be revised according to progress.","mnemonic":"PGOIR: Problem, Goal, Objectives, Interventions, Review.","id":"process-treatment-plan","category":"process"},{"label":"Case formulation","cue":"an organized explanation linking the client's presenting problems with relevant history, triggers, maintaining factors, strengths and a rationale for intervention","explanation":"Formulation goes beyond a diagnosis by explaining why this person is struggling in this way at this time and what may help.","mnemonic":"5P option: Presenting, Predisposing, Precipitating, Perpetuating, Protective.","id":"process-case-formulation","category":"process"},{"label":"Progress review","cue":"evaluating whether objectives are being achieved and modifying the treatment plan when progress is absent, circumstances change or goals are met","explanation":"Treatment planning is dynamic; review links evidence of progress to continuation, modification or termination.","mnemonic":"Review = measure, discuss, revise.","id":"process-progress-review","category":"process"}],"truefalse":[{"id":"tf-psychopath-1","statement":"A panic attack can occur in disorders other than panic disorder.","answer":true,"explanation":"A panic attack is a symptom episode and can occur across several disorders; panic disorder requires a recurrent pattern plus ongoing concern or behavior change.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"tf-psychopath-2","statement":"Obsessions are repetitive behaviors, whereas compulsions are intrusive thoughts.","answer":false,"explanation":"The relationship is reversed: obsessions are intrusive thoughts/urges/images; compulsions are repetitive behaviors or mental acts.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"tf-psychopath-3","statement":"Trauma exposure can increase risk for depression, anxiety and substance-related problems.","answer":true,"explanation":"Trauma can be followed by several forms of psychopathology, not only PTSD.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"tf-psychopath-4","statement":"A single laboratory test confirms schizophrenia.","answer":false,"explanation":"Schizophrenia is diagnosed clinically from symptoms, course, duration and impairment while ruling out other causes.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"tf-psychopath-5","statement":"In major depression, loss of interest or pleasure is called anhedonia.","answer":true,"explanation":"Anhedonia is a core depressive symptom.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"tf-psychopath-6","statement":"Reduced need for sleep, grandiosity and pressured speech are compatible with mania.","answer":true,"explanation":"Those are classic manic activation features.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"tf-psychopath-7","statement":"Bulimia nervosa necessarily requires markedly low body weight.","answer":false,"explanation":"Markedly low weight is central to anorexia; bulimia can occur at many body weights.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"tf-psychopath-8","statement":"Binge-eating disorder is defined by recurrent binge eating without regular compensatory behaviors.","answer":true,"explanation":"Regular purging or other compensation would point more toward bulimia if other criteria are met.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"tf-psychopath-9","statement":"OCPD and OCD are identical diagnoses.","answer":false,"explanation":"OCPD is a personality pattern of perfectionism/control; OCD centers on obsessions and compulsions.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"tf-psychopath-10","statement":"Borderline personality disorder may include abandonment fears and recurrent self-injury.","answer":true,"explanation":"Both are characteristic features of borderline personality disorder.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"tf-psychopath-11","statement":"Schizoid personality disorder is defined primarily by intense fear of criticism and rejection.","answer":false,"explanation":"Schizoid personality is primarily social detachment with limited desire for close relationships.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"tf-psychopath-12","statement":"Autism spectrum disorder includes both social-communication difficulties and restricted/repetitive behavior patterns.","answer":true,"explanation":"Both domains are required in the modern diagnostic framework.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"tf-psychopath-13","statement":"The four D framework can include deviance, distress, dysfunction and danger.","answer":true,"explanation":"These four dimensions are commonly taught as aids for thinking about abnormality.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"tf-psychopath-14","statement":"Comorbidity means that two or more disorders can occur in the same person.","answer":true,"explanation":"Comorbidity is the co-occurrence of disorders.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"tf-psychopath-15","statement":"A specific phobia is generalized worry across many areas of life.","answer":false,"explanation":"Generalized worry across many domains is more characteristic of GAD.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"tf-psychopath-16","statement":"PTSD is diagnosed solely because a frightening event occurred.","answer":false,"explanation":"Trauma exposure is necessary but not sufficient; a characteristic symptom pattern and duration/impact are also required.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"tf-psychopath-17","statement":"Dependent personality disorder may involve difficulty making everyday decisions without reassurance.","answer":true,"explanation":"Excessive reliance on others is a central feature.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"tf-psychopath-18","statement":"An opioid is more likely than a stimulant to produce drowsiness and physiologic slowing.","answer":true,"explanation":"Opioids commonly produce sedation, whereas stimulants activate.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"tf-psychopath-19","statement":"A manic episode is simply a happy mood with no change in energy or functioning.","answer":false,"explanation":"Mania involves abnormal mood plus increased energy/activity and additional symptoms with significant impact.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"tf-psychopath-20","statement":"Anorexia nervosa can include binge-eating/purging behavior if the person also meets the low-weight anorexia criteria.","answer":true,"explanation":"That is the binge-eating/purging subtype of anorexia nervosa.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"tf-techniques-1","statement":"Accurate empathy, unconditional positive regard and congruence are core person-centered conditions.","answer":true,"explanation":"These are the classic Rogers conditions emphasized in person-centered therapy.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"tf-techniques-2","statement":"Albert Ellis is the major proponent of Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy.","answer":true,"explanation":"Ellis developed REBT.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"tf-techniques-3","statement":"Sigmund Freud is the foundational proponent of psychoanalytic therapy.","answer":true,"explanation":"Freud developed psychoanalysis.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"tf-techniques-4","statement":"Free association requires the therapist to censor embarrassing client material.","answer":false,"explanation":"The client is invited to say what comes to mind without censorship.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"tf-techniques-5","statement":"Transference refers mainly to the therapist transferring personal feelings onto the client.","answer":false,"explanation":"That description is closer to countertransference; transference is the client carrying past relationship patterns into the therapeutic relationship.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"tf-techniques-6","statement":"Negative reinforcement weakens behavior.","answer":false,"explanation":"All reinforcement strengthens behavior; negative reinforcement does so by removing an aversive condition.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"tf-techniques-7","statement":"Extinction involves withholding the reinforcement that has maintained a behavior.","answer":true,"explanation":"When reinforcement stops, the behavior may gradually decrease.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"tf-techniques-8","statement":"Systematic desensitization classically combines relaxation with graded exposure.","answer":true,"explanation":"The client learns relaxation and moves through a fear hierarchy.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"tf-techniques-9","statement":"Flooding is always more gradual than systematic desensitization.","answer":false,"explanation":"Flooding is deliberately high-intensity and comparatively rapid.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"tf-techniques-10","statement":"Gestalt therapy emphasizes present-moment awareness.","answer":true,"explanation":"Here-and-now awareness is central to Gestalt work.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"tf-techniques-11","statement":"The empty-chair technique is commonly associated with Gestalt therapy.","answer":true,"explanation":"It is a classic Gestalt experiment for unfinished business or polarities.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"tf-techniques-12","statement":"Existential therapy aims to eliminate every form of anxiety from life.","answer":false,"explanation":"Existential therapy accepts that some anxiety is inherent in freedom, responsibility and existence; the aim is authentic engagement, not zero anxiety.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"tf-techniques-13","statement":"Adlerian therapy emphasizes purposeful, goal-directed behavior and personal responsibility.","answer":true,"explanation":"Those ideas are explicitly emphasized in the course audio opening.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"tf-techniques-14","statement":"CBT assumes thoughts, emotions and behaviors can influence one another.","answer":true,"explanation":"This reciprocal link is a core CBT formulation.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"tf-techniques-15","statement":"DBT teaches mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness.","answer":true,"explanation":"These are the four major DBT skills domains.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"tf-techniques-16","statement":"Progressive muscle relaxation involves systematically increasing tension and never releasing it.","answer":false,"explanation":"The method alternates tension with deliberate release and relaxation.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"tf-techniques-17","statement":"In vivo exposure occurs only in imagination.","answer":false,"explanation":"In vivo exposure takes place in the real feared situation or with the real feared object.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"tf-techniques-18","statement":"Retroflection involves directing toward oneself an impulse that might otherwise be directed outward.","answer":true,"explanation":"That is the Gestalt meaning of retroflection.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"tf-techniques-19","statement":"Rational emotive imagery belongs to the REBT family of techniques.","answer":true,"explanation":"It is used to rehearse changing unhealthy emotion by changing beliefs.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"tf-techniques-20","statement":"Social skills training can use modeling, role play, rehearsal and feedback.","answer":true,"explanation":"Those are common behavioral teaching components.","category":"techniques"}],"shortanswers":[{"id":"short-psychopath-1","question":"Differentiate a panic attack from panic disorder.","marks":2,"points":["A panic attack is an acute episode of intense fear/discomfort with physical/cognitive symptoms.","Panic disorder requires recurrent unexpected attacks plus persistent concern and/or maladaptive behavior change."],"mnemonic":"Attack = episode; disorder = recurring pattern + aftermath.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"short-psychopath-2","question":"Differentiate obsession from compulsion and give one example of each.","marks":2,"points":["Obsession: recurrent intrusive unwanted thought/urge/image, e.g. fear of contamination.","Compulsion: repetitive behavior/mental act to reduce distress, e.g. repeated hand washing."],"mnemonic":"O = thought; C = conduct.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"short-psychopath-3","question":"State the four D criteria commonly used when considering abnormality.","marks":2,"points":["Deviance","Distress","Dysfunction","Danger"],"mnemonic":"4D: Deviance, Distress, Dysfunction, Danger.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"short-psychopath-4","question":"Define substance use disorder in practical clinical terms.","marks":2,"points":["Problematic pattern of substance use causing significant impairment or distress.","Look for loss of control, social/role impairment, risky use and/or tolerance/withdrawal."],"mnemonic":"4C: Control loss, Consequences, Craving, Continued use.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"short-psychopath-5","question":"Give two reasons early intervention in substance use disorder is important.","marks":2,"points":["Reduces medical, social, occupational and legal harm and risk of escalation.","Improves opportunity for engagement, relapse prevention, family support and recovery before consequences accumulate."],"mnemonic":"EARLY = less Entrenchment, Address Risk, Link help, Yield recovery.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"short-psychopath-6","question":"Differentiate anorexia nervosa from bulimia nervosa.","marks":2,"points":["Anorexia requires significantly low body weight with restriction/fear of weight gain/body-image disturbance.","Bulimia centers on recurrent binge eating plus compensatory behavior; markedly low weight is not required."],"mnemonic":"Low weight points strongly toward anorexia.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"short-psychopath-7","question":"State two characteristic features of a manic episode.","marks":2,"points":["Reduced need for sleep, grandiosity, pressured speech, flight of ideas, increased goal-directed activity, risky behavior - any two."],"mnemonic":"DIG FAST.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"short-psychopath-8","question":"What is comorbidity?","marks":2,"points":["The presence of two or more disorders in the same person at the same time or over an overlapping period."],"mnemonic":"CO-morbidity = conditions CO-occur.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"short-psychopath-9","question":"Name the four broad PTSD symptom clusters taught in modern formulations.","marks":2,"points":["Intrusion","Avoidance","Negative changes in cognition/mood","Arousal/reactivity"],"mnemonic":"IANA.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"short-psychopath-10","question":"What is meant by anhedonia?","marks":2,"points":["Marked loss of interest or pleasure in activities previously enjoyed; a core depressive symptom."],"mnemonic":"AN-hedonia = absence of pleasure.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"short-psychopath-11","question":"Differentiate OCD from obsessive-compulsive personality disorder.","marks":2,"points":["OCD involves obsessions and/or compulsions causing distress/time burden.","OCPD is a pervasive pattern of perfectionism, orderliness and control; obsessions/compulsions are not required."],"mnemonic":"OCD = obsessions/compulsions; OCPD = order/control/personality.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"short-psychopath-12","question":"Give two signs that suggest psychosis.","marks":2,"points":["Examples: delusions, hallucinations, markedly disorganized speech/behavior, impaired reality testing."],"mnemonic":"3D: Delusions, perceptual Disturbance, Disorganization.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"short-psychopath-13","question":"Why must suicide risk be assessed in a severely depressed client?","marks":2,"points":["Depression may involve hopelessness and suicidal ideation; assessment identifies immediacy of risk and need for protective intervention/referral."],"mnemonic":"Ask directly about thoughts, plan, intent, means and protective factors.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"short-psychopath-14","question":"State two differences between GAD and a specific phobia.","marks":2,"points":["GAD: diffuse worry across multiple domains; specific phobia: circumscribed fear of a particular object/situation.","GAD is persistent generalized worry; phobia is triggered by the feared stimulus and avoidance."],"mnemonic":"General vs specific.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"short-psychopath-15","question":"What does DSM refer to in psychopathology?","marks":2,"points":["Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders.","A classification system used to describe diagnostic criteria; it supports but does not replace clinical assessment."],"mnemonic":"DSM = Diagnostic + Statistical + Manual.","category":"psychopath"},{"id":"short-techniques-1","question":"Describe the empty-chair technique.","marks":2,"points":["Place an empty chair as if a significant person or part of self were present.","Client speaks directly to that chair and may switch roles, exploring unfinished feelings in the here-and-now."],"mnemonic":"Empty chair makes the absent dialogue present.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"short-techniques-2","question":"Describe progressive muscle relaxation.","marks":2,"points":["Systematically tense a muscle group briefly while noticing tension.","Release and notice relaxation, then proceed through major muscle groups with calm breathing."],"mnemonic":"Tense - notice - release.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"short-techniques-3","question":"Define free association.","marks":2,"points":["Client verbalizes whatever comes to mind without censorship, however trivial or uncomfortable, to help reveal patterns and unconscious material."],"mnemonic":"Free means uncensored.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"short-techniques-4","question":"State two core conditions of person-centered therapy.","marks":2,"points":["Any two: accurate empathy, unconditional positive regard, congruence/genuineness."],"mnemonic":"EUC.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"short-techniques-5","question":"Give the ABC elements of REBT.","marks":2,"points":["A = Activating event","B = Belief about the event","C = emotional/behavioral Consequence"],"mnemonic":"ABC: event does not mechanically create emotion; beliefs mediate.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"short-techniques-6","question":"Explain negative reinforcement with an example.","marks":2,"points":["Negative reinforcement increases a behavior by removing an aversive stimulus.","Example: fastening a seat belt to stop the warning sound makes future seat-belt fastening more likely."],"mnemonic":"Reinforcement increases; negative removes.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"short-techniques-7","question":"What is transference?","marks":2,"points":["Client projects/re-enacts feelings, expectations or patterns from earlier important relationships onto the therapist."],"mnemonic":"Client TRANSfers old patterns.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"short-techniques-8","question":"What is countertransference?","marks":2,"points":["Therapist emotional response to the client, especially where the therapist's own history or vulnerabilities shape that response; requires awareness/supervision."],"mnemonic":"Counter = counselor side.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"short-techniques-9","question":"State one goal of existential therapy.","marks":1,"points":["Help the client face freedom/responsibility, clarify meaning and values, make authentic choices and live more deliberately - any one."],"mnemonic":"FMRC.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"short-techniques-10","question":"State one technique specific to psychoanalytic work.","marks":1,"points":["Free association, interpretation, analysis of resistance, transference analysis or dream analysis - any one."],"mnemonic":"FIRT.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"short-techniques-11","question":"Explain systematic desensitization in three steps.","marks":2,"points":["Teach relaxation/coping response.","Build a graded fear hierarchy.","While relaxed, expose progressively from easier to harder items until anxiety reduces."],"mnemonic":"RHE.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"short-techniques-12","question":"Distinguish flooding from graded exposure.","marks":2,"points":["Flooding begins with high-intensity feared material and sustains exposure.","Graded exposure moves stepwise from lower to higher fear levels."],"mnemonic":"Flooding = deep end; graded = ladder.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"short-techniques-13","question":"What is cognitive restructuring?","marks":2,"points":["Identify unhelpful/biased thoughts, examine evidence, and generate more balanced alternatives, then test them behaviorally when appropriate."],"mnemonic":"Catch - Check - Change.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"short-techniques-14","question":"State two principles emphasized in Adlerian therapy.","marks":2,"points":["Any two: behavior is purposeful/goal-directed; social relatedness/social interest; conscious choice; responsibility; lifestyle; encouragement."],"mnemonic":"GSCR.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"short-techniques-15","question":"What does extinction mean in behavior therapy?","marks":1,"points":["A reinforced behavior decreases when the reinforcement maintaining it is consistently withheld."],"mnemonic":"Extinguish the payoff.","category":"techniques"},{"id":"short-ethics-1","question":"What does duty to warn/protect mean in therapy?","marks":1,"points":["When a serious credible threat to an identifiable person is present, the therapist may have to take reasonable protective action according to applicable law and ethics, even though confidentiality is normally protected."],"mnemonic":"TIP: Threat, Identifiable person, Protect.","category":"ethics"},{"id":"short-ethics-2","question":"Define mandated reporting.","marks":1,"points":["A legal/professional requirement to report specified situations such as suspected abuse to designated authorities; it is a limit to confidentiality."],"mnemonic":"Mandatory = must report when the law says so.","category":"ethics"},{"id":"short-ethics-3","question":"State two ways a therapist should manage sexual or romantic feelings toward a client.","marks":2,"points":["Acknowledge and monitor the feelings rather than act on them.","Seek supervision/consultation and strengthen boundaries; refer if objectivity/competence cannot be maintained."],"mnemonic":"FEEL, do not ACT: notice, supervise, protect boundaries.","category":"ethics"},{"id":"short-ethics-4","question":"What should a therapist do when credible serious unethical behavior by a colleague is suspected?","marks":1,"points":["Clarify facts, consult the code/supervisor as appropriate, protect the client and follow formal reporting procedures when required."],"mnemonic":"CARE: Consult, Assess, Report, Ensure safety.","category":"ethics"},{"id":"short-ethics-5","question":"Differentiate privacy from confidentiality.","marks":2,"points":["Privacy is the client's right to control access to self and personal information.","Confidentiality is the professional duty to protect information obtained in the professional relationship."],"mnemonic":"Privacy = person's right; confidentiality = professional's duty.","category":"ethics"},{"id":"short-ethics-6","question":"Differentiate confidentiality from privileged communication.","marks":2,"points":["Confidentiality is an ethical/professional duty.","Privilege is a legal evidentiary protection against compelled disclosure in certain proceedings and depends on jurisdiction."],"mnemonic":"Privilege = courtroom shield.","category":"ethics"},{"id":"short-ethics-7","question":"Define malpractice.","marks":1,"points":["Professional negligence in which a duty is breached and the breach causes harm; commonly considered through duty, breach, causation and damages/harm."],"mnemonic":"DBHC.","category":"ethics"},{"id":"short-ethics-8","question":"Give two elements of informed consent.","marks":2,"points":["Examples: nature/purpose of services, risks, benefits, alternatives, fees, therapist qualifications, limits of confidentiality, voluntary choice, right to ask questions."],"mnemonic":"BRALC.","category":"ethics"},{"id":"short-ethics-9","question":"When is termination ethically appropriate?","marks":1,"points":["When goals are met, treatment is no longer beneficial/needed, the therapist cannot provide competent service, or other legitimate reasons exist - with adequate planning to avoid abandonment."],"mnemonic":"PLAN.","category":"ethics"},{"id":"short-ethics-10","question":"State one key ethical requirement for clinical records.","marks":1,"points":["Records should be accurate, timely, relevant and securely stored with access protected."],"mnemonic":"ACTS.","category":"ethics"},{"id":"short-ethics-11","question":"Why are dual relationships ethically risky?","marks":2,"points":["They can impair objectivity or professional judgment.","They can increase risk of exploitation, coercion, conflicts of interest or harm."],"mnemonic":"Power + objectivity + exploitation.","category":"ethics"},{"id":"short-ethics-12","question":"What is a boundary violation?","marks":1,"points":["A serious harmful or exploitative breach of therapeutic boundaries."],"mnemonic":"Violation = harm/exploitation.","category":"ethics"},{"id":"short-ethics-13","question":"State two technology-related ethical concerns in teletherapy.","marks":2,"points":["Examples: data security/privacy, informed consent, identity verification, competence with technology, emergency location/plan, jurisdiction."],"mnemonic":"SPACE.","category":"ethics"},{"id":"short-ethics-14","question":"How should a therapist respond to a subpoena for records?","marks":2,"points":["Do not reflexively release the entire file.","Consult applicable law/professional guidance and the client/attorney; protect confidentiality and disclose only what is authorized or legally required."],"mnemonic":"STOP.","category":"ethics"},{"id":"short-ethics-15","question":"What is the principle of justice?","marks":1,"points":["Fair and equitable treatment and distribution of benefits, burdens and resources."],"mnemonic":"Justice = fairness.","category":"ethics"},{"id":"short-process-1","question":"Outline two benefits of group therapy.","marks":4,"points":["Reduces isolation/universality: members learn they are not alone.","Provides mutual support and feedback.","Allows interpersonal learning, modeling and practice.","Can be efficient and increase access - any two well explained."],"mnemonic":"U-S-M: Universality, Support, Modeling.","category":"process"},{"id":"short-process-2","question":"State two characteristics of a culturally competent therapist.","marks":4,"points":["Self-awareness of own values/biases.","Knowledge of different cultural contexts and humility about what is not known.","Adaptable communication/clinical skills.","Willingness to seek cultural encounters/consultation and avoid imposition - any two explained."],"mnemonic":"AKSE.","category":"process"},{"id":"short-process-3","question":"What is the counseling process?","marks":2,"points":["A collaborative sequence that typically includes engagement/rapport, assessment, goal setting, intervention, review, and termination/follow-up."],"mnemonic":"RAGIRT: Rapport, Assessment, Goals, Intervention, Review, Termination.","category":"process"},{"id":"short-process-4","question":"Differentiate psychoeducational groups from skills-development groups.","marks":4,"points":["Psychoeducational groups emphasize teaching information/understanding about a problem or condition.","Skills groups emphasize practicing specific behaviors or coping/interpersonal skills with rehearsal and feedback."],"mnemonic":"Education = know; skills = do.","category":"process"},{"id":"short-process-5","question":"Define a treatment plan and list four common elements.","marks":4,"points":["A collaborative roadmap tailored to the client linking assessment/formulation to care.","Common elements: identified problem(s), goals, measurable objectives, interventions/strategies, responsible persons/frequency as relevant, review/progress criteria."],"mnemonic":"PGOIR.","category":"process"},{"id":"short-process-6","question":"Explain SMART goals.","marks":4,"points":["Specific","Measurable","Achievable","Relevant/Realistic","Time-bound"],"mnemonic":"SMART.","category":"process"},{"id":"short-process-7","question":"Differentiate paraphrasing from reflection of feeling.","marks":4,"points":["Paraphrasing restates the content/meaning in new words.","Reflection of feeling identifies and feeds back the emotion or emotional meaning."],"mnemonic":"Paraphrase = facts/meaning; reflection = feeling.","category":"process"},{"id":"short-process-8","question":"Why should a qualified interpreter be preferred to a child family member?","marks":4,"points":["Improves accuracy and reduces role burden on the child.","Better supports confidentiality, informed consent and sensitive disclosure."],"mnemonic":"Accuracy + confidentiality + appropriate role.","category":"process"},{"id":"short-process-9","question":"State four tasks of ethical termination.","marks":4,"points":["Review progress and goals.","Discuss feelings and reasons for ending.","Consolidate skills/relapse prevention.","Arrange follow-up/referral/continuity when needed."],"mnemonic":"RECAP.","category":"process"},{"id":"short-process-10","question":"What is case formulation?","marks":4,"points":["An organized explanation of how presenting problems connect with history, triggers, maintaining factors and strengths/protective factors.","It guides treatment by explaining why the problem developed/persists and what interventions make sense."],"mnemonic":"5P is one useful organizing mnemonic.","category":"process"},{"id":"short-process-11","question":"Describe the storming and norming stages of group development.","marks":4,"points":["Storming: conflict, resistance, competition and testing of roles/leader.","Norming: cohesion, shared norms, trust and cooperation become more stable."],"mnemonic":"Storm -> Norm.","category":"process"},{"id":"short-process-12","question":"Give four examples of counseling skills used in an intake session.","marks":4,"points":["Examples: active listening, empathy, open-ended questions, reflection, paraphrasing, summarizing, clarification, therapeutic silence, appropriate confrontation."],"mnemonic":"LEAP: Listen, Empathize, Ask, Paraphrase.","category":"process"}],"cases":[{"id":"case-01","title":"Work stress and first counseling session","scenario":"Amina, 24, works as a teacher and has recently been working 11-12 hour days. they has become irritable with colleagues, sleeps poorly, has stopped seeing friends and says, \"I am losing control of my life.\" Amina has never attended counseling before and is unsure what to expect.","question":"Carry out the first counseling session with this client. Bring out the counseling skills, assessment priorities and initial treatment planning steps you would use. (25mks)","approach":"First-session counseling process with person-centered engagement plus problem-focused assessment","points":["Establish rapport, privacy and a respectful therapeutic setting.","Explain role, informed consent and limits of confidentiality.","Use open questions, active listening, paraphrase, reflection and summarizing.","Assess stressors, sleep, functioning, substance use, supports, medical/mental-health history and risk including suicide/self-harm.","Identify strengths and the client's own priorities.","Agree on a preliminary formulation: overload, reduced recovery, irritability and impaired balance; avoid premature diagnosis.","Set one or two SMART initial goals.","Introduce an appropriate first coping intervention such as breathing/relaxation, activity scheduling, boundaries or problem solving.","Agree on homework/self-monitoring and next appointment.","Close by summarizing, checking understanding and inviting questions."],"mnemonic":"RAPPORT: Rapport, Agreement/consent, Problem assessment, Prioritize risk, Objectives, Response/technique, Take-home/follow-up."},{"id":"case-02","title":"Driving phobia","scenario":"Brenda, 31, stopped driving after a serious road accident many years ago. As a passenger they develops sweating, shaking, light-headedness and an urge to escape, except when a trusted relative drives and talks calmly. A new job now requires independent travel and Brenda wants to regain driving confidence.","question":"What therapeutic approach/technique would you use? Explain in detail how you would apply it from assessment to graded practice. (25mks)","approach":"Behavior therapy - graded exposure/systematic desensitization, with CBT elements as needed","points":["Assess the fear, accident memory, panic symptoms, avoidance, current driving competence and safety.","Explain the fear-avoidance cycle and rationale for exposure.","Teach a coping/relaxation skill without turning it into avoidance.","Build a hierarchy from low to high fear (e.g., sitting in parked car, engine on, empty road, short familiar route, busier road).","Use repeated in vivo graded exposure, remaining in each step long enough for learning.","Reduce safety behaviors gradually while keeping actual road safety.","Identify and test catastrophic thoughts such as \"I will certainly crash\" or \"anxiety means danger.\"","Use homework and record anxiety/predictions/outcomes.","Reinforce progress and move up the hierarchy collaboratively.","Plan relapse prevention and future independent practice."],"mnemonic":"RHE: Relax/coping, Hierarchy, Exposure - then Repeat and Review."},{"id":"case-03","title":"Depression after loss","scenario":"For the past three weeks, Carlos, 38, has felt sad nearly every day after losing a job/relationship. they reports loss of interest, fatigue, poor concentration, reduced appetite, insomnia and thoughts such as \"I am useless and nothing will improve.\"","question":"Identify the likely disorder and demonstrate a CBT-oriented session, including assessment, safety and techniques. (25mks)","approach":"Major depressive presentation - CBT with safety assessment and behavioral activation","points":["Recognize a depressive syndrome and assess duration, severity, impairment and differentials.","Ask directly about suicidal thoughts, plan, intent, means and protective factors; escalate/referral if risk is acute.","Collaboratively map situation-thought-emotion-behavior links.","Identify distortions such as overgeneralization, labeling and hopeless predictions.","Use evidence for/against and balanced alternative thoughts.","Introduce graded behavioral activation beginning with small achievable activities.","Address sleep/routine and social support.","Set SMART goals and a simple monitoring sheet.","Coordinate medical/psychiatric assessment when severity, suicidality or medication questions require it.","Review learning and homework."],"mnemonic":"CBT TEA + SIGECAPS; safety first."},{"id":"case-04","title":"Bereavement and person-centered counseling","scenario":"Daniel, 45, recently lost a parent/spouse and arrives tearful, disheveled and unsure whether the intensity of grief is \"normal.\" they says family members keep telling them to \"move on,\" which has made them stop talking about the loss.","question":"Demonstrate how a person-centered therapist would conduct the session. Include the core conditions and appropriate assessment. (25mks)","approach":"Person-centered therapy with grief assessment","points":["Create safety and let the client set the pace.","Use accurate empathy and reflective listening.","Offer unconditional positive regard/nonjudgment.","Be congruent/genuine without making the session about the therapist.","Invite the client to describe the meaning of the loss and present emotions.","Avoid premature advice, reassurance or forced positivity.","Assess functioning, supports and safety, including suicidal ideation where indicated.","Differentiate normal grief from a depressive disorder through duration, pervasive anhedonia/hopelessness and functional impairment without pathologizing grief automatically.","Help the client identify needs and supports.","Summarize and collaboratively decide next steps."],"mnemonic":"EUC: Empathy, Unconditional positive regard, Congruence."},{"id":"case-05","title":"Generalized anxiety and worry","scenario":"Esther, 52, worries most days about finances, health, family and work. The worry has persisted for many months, is difficult to stop, and is accompanied by muscle tension, restlessness and poor sleep. they repeatedly seeks reassurance but remains anxious.","question":"Give the most likely diagnosis and show how CBT and relaxation/coping methods could be used. (25mks)","approach":"GAD - CBT with cognitive restructuring, worry management and relaxation","points":["Assess breadth, duration and controllability of worry and rule out substance/medical contributors.","Formulate the cycle of threat prediction, reassurance/avoidance and short-lived relief.","Use psychoeducation about anxiety and uncertainty.","Identify probability overestimation and catastrophic beliefs.","Use cognitive restructuring and behavioral experiments.","Teach breathing or progressive muscle relaxation for arousal.","Consider scheduled worry/problem-solving time and reduction of reassurance seeking.","Set graded behavioral goals and homework.","Review sleep, caffeine/substance use and supports.","Monitor outcome and adjust plan."],"mnemonic":"GAD = General Anxiety across Domains; CBT = Catch, Check, Change."},{"id":"case-06","title":"Panic with avoidance","scenario":"Fidel, 59, has recurrent sudden episodes of racing heart, sweating, breathlessness and fear of dying. After several attacks in public places, they now avoids buses, markets and long queues and only goes out with a companion.","question":"Formulate the problem and demonstrate a CBT/exposure treatment plan. (25mks)","approach":"Panic disorder with agoraphobic avoidance - CBT and exposure","points":["Assess attack features, medical/substance causes, frequency, anticipatory anxiety and avoidance.","Explain the panic cycle and misinterpretation of bodily sensations.","Teach realistic reappraisal of sensations.","Use interoceptive exposure where appropriate to feared bodily sensations.","Build graded in vivo exposure to avoided settings.","Reduce safety behaviors gradually.","Use breathing skills carefully as coping, not as proof that anxiety is dangerous.","Track predictions and actual outcomes.","Assess risk and comorbidity.","Plan relapse prevention."],"mnemonic":"Panic: BODY sensation -> catastrophic THOUGHT -> FEAR -> avoidance; exposure breaks the loop."},{"id":"case-07","title":"OCD contamination and washing","scenario":"Grace, 28, spends several hours each day washing hands and cleaning after intrusive fears of contamination. they knows the rituals are excessive but believes that not washing could cause serious harm to family members.","question":"What treatment technique is most appropriate? Demonstrate how you would use it safely and collaboratively. (25mks)","approach":"CBT with Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP)","points":["Clarify obsessions, compulsions, triggers, time burden and insight.","Explain how rituals reduce anxiety briefly and thereby maintain OCD.","Develop a graded exposure hierarchy collaboratively.","Expose to feared contamination cues in planned steps.","Prevent or delay the washing ritual during/after exposure.","Remain long enough for new learning rather than immediate escape.","Address inflated responsibility/catastrophic beliefs with cognitive methods as needed.","Do not provide repeated reassurance that becomes part of the compulsion.","Use homework and repeated practice.","Monitor distress, safety and progress."],"mnemonic":"ERP = Expose + Resist ritual + Practice."},{"id":"case-08","title":"Social anxiety and skills practice","scenario":"Hassan, 35, avoids speaking in meetings and meeting new people because of fear of blushing, stumbling over words and being judged as incompetent. This is beginning to limit career and friendships.","question":"Demonstrate a treatment plan combining CBT/exposure with social-skills methods where indicated. (25mks)","approach":"Social anxiety - CBT, graded exposure and social skills training if deficits are present","points":["Assess feared situations, predictions, avoidance and actual skill deficits.","Explain self-focused attention and avoidance/safety behaviors.","Identify negative predictions about judgment.","Build graded social/performance exposures.","Use behavioral experiments and reduce safety behaviors.","If skill deficits exist, use instruction, modeling, role play, rehearsal and feedback.","Practice attention shifting toward the interaction.","Set homework in real social situations.","Review outcomes against predictions.","Plan maintenance and relapse prevention."],"mnemonic":"IMRF for skills: Instruction, Modeling, Rehearsal, Feedback."},{"id":"case-09","title":"Trauma symptoms and stabilization","scenario":"After a violent incident, Irene, 42, experiences nightmares, intrusive memories, avoidance, hypervigilance and episodes of feeling detached or unreal. they becomes overwhelmed when asked to describe the event in detail.","question":"Formulate the presentation and demonstrate an appropriate early trauma-focused session, explaining why pacing matters. (25mks)","approach":"PTSD/trauma presentation - safety, grounding and stabilization before intensive processing when dissociation is high","points":["Assess trauma symptoms, duration, functioning, suicide/self-harm risk, substance use and current safety.","Recognize intrusion, avoidance, negative mood/cognition and arousal; assess dissociation.","Explain trauma responses and normalize without minimizing.","Use grounding/orientation to present, paced breathing and stabilization skills.","Establish a window of tolerable arousal and stop/modify processing if dissociation increases.","Identify supports and coping resources.","Collaboratively set goals and consent for later trauma processing.","Use evidence-based trauma-focused treatment only when sufficiently stable and within competence.","Coordinate psychiatric/medical care where needed.","End with reorientation and a clear safety/follow-up plan."],"mnemonic":"SAFER: Safety, Assess, Frame/psychoeducate, Establish grounding, Review readiness."},{"id":"case-10","title":"Adolescent substance use","scenario":"Joel, age 16, has begun using pills and alcohol several times a week after being bullied and reports skipping school. The adolescent says, \"It is the only thing that calms me down,\" and is unsure about stopping.","question":"Conduct an initial intervention. Include risk assessment, confidentiality limits, motivation and referral/treatment planning. (25mks)","approach":"Substance-use assessment with motivational, supportive and safety-focused intervention","points":["Explain confidentiality and its limits for a minor in the relevant setting.","Assess substances, quantity/frequency, intoxication/withdrawal, overdose risk, source, polysubstance use and impairment.","Assess suicide/self-harm, violence, exploitation and bullying safety.","Explore pros/cons and ambivalence without confrontation or shaming.","Link use to triggers and functions such as coping with bullying.","Elicit the client's own reasons for change and strengths.","Provide age-appropriate psychoeducation/harm reduction within scope.","Involve caregivers/other professionals according to consent, safety and legal requirements.","Refer for specialist substance/medical evaluation when indicated.","Create a concrete follow-up and safety plan."],"mnemonic":"ASK: Assess use/risk, Seek motivation, Keep safety/referral central."},{"id":"case-11","title":"Psychosis and supportive psychotherapy","scenario":"Kemi, 56, has a history of psychosis and recently began sleeping very little, withdrawing from family and saying that neighbors are sending coded messages through the radio. they is suspicious but willing to talk.","question":"Show how you would conduct a safe supportive session and coordinate care. (25mks)","approach":"Psychosis - supportive engagement, risk assessment and psychiatric collaboration","points":["Use calm, respectful engagement and simple clear communication.","Assess hallucinations/delusions, disorganization, sleep, medication adherence, substance use and functional decline.","Assess suicide, violence, command hallucinations, inability to care for self and acute medical risk.","Do not ridicule or aggressively argue with the belief; validate emotion without confirming the delusion.","Orient to shared reality gently and focus on distress/coping.","Encourage medication/psychiatric review and coordinate with the treatment team.","Mobilize family/supports with appropriate consent and safety considerations.","Use simple coping and routine goals.","Escalate to emergency care if risk or severe decompensation requires it.","Document assessment and plan."],"mnemonic":"CALM: Connect, Assess risk, Listen without endorsing delusion, Mobilize care."},{"id":"case-12","title":"Eating disorder assessment","scenario":"Lucas, 25, has lost substantial weight, restricts food, fears weight gain and exercises excessively. Family members report dizziness and fainting. Lucas insists there is no problem.","question":"Identify the likely disorder and outline a safe psychotherapy assessment and treatment approach. (25mks)","approach":"Anorexia nervosa presentation - multidisciplinary assessment plus psychotherapy","points":["Recognize low-weight restriction/fear of weight gain as consistent with anorexia nervosa.","Assess eating patterns, compensatory behavior, body image, exercise, weight trajectory and motivation.","Assess medical instability urgently (fainting, dehydration, cardiac risk) and refer for medical evaluation.","Assess suicide/self-harm and comorbid depression/anxiety.","Use nonjudgmental engagement; avoid power struggles over food.","Use psychoeducation and motivational work.","Coordinate nutritional/medical care and family involvement where appropriate.","Use evidence-based psychotherapy within competence (e.g., CBT-oriented methods/family-based approaches depending on age/context).","Set measurable behavioral and psychological goals.","Monitor progress and medical risk closely."],"mnemonic":"MIND: Medical risk, Intake/eating pattern, Negative body beliefs, Danger/suicide."},{"id":"case-13","title":"Emotion dysregulation and self-harm","scenario":"Martha, 32, describes rapidly shifting intense relationships, fear of abandonment, impulsive spending, recurrent self-cutting and episodes of overwhelming anger followed by shame.","question":"What therapeutic model is particularly relevant? Demonstrate the priorities and skills you would introduce. (25mks)","approach":"Borderline personality features - DBT-informed treatment with safety planning","points":["Assess current self-harm/suicide risk first and establish a safety/crisis plan.","Validate suffering while maintaining clear boundaries.","Explain the DBT balance of acceptance and change.","Teach mindfulness of current emotion/urge.","Introduce distress-tolerance alternatives to self-harm.","Teach emotion-regulation skills and trigger tracking.","Address interpersonal effectiveness and abandonment-related behaviors.","Use behavioral chain analysis around a recent self-harm episode.","Set treatment hierarchy and collaborative goals.","Coordinate psychiatric/medical care when indicated."],"mnemonic":"MDEI: Mindfulness, Distress tolerance, Emotion regulation, Interpersonal effectiveness."},{"id":"case-14","title":"Authority, guilt and independence","scenario":"Nadine, 39, grew up in a strict family and school environment. they automatically seeks approval from authority figures, feels guilty when disagreeing, and wants to make independent adult choices but fears rejection and moral failure.","question":"Choose an anchor therapeutic approach and demonstrate a session that addresses choice, responsibility, values and assertiveness. (25mks)","approach":"Existential and/or Adlerian formulation, with assertiveness practice","points":["Build an accepting relationship and explore the client's lived experience.","Clarify inherited values versus personally chosen values without imposing the therapist's values.","Explore freedom, responsibility and anxiety that accompany choice.","Identify life goals and patterns of approval-seeking.","Adlerian option: examine lifestyle/private logic and use encouragement toward social interest and responsible choice.","Existential option: explore authenticity, meaning and willingness to tolerate guilt/anxiety of choosing.","Practice assertive statements/role play for a realistic situation.","Set a small behavioral experiment in independent decision-making.","Explore cultural/family context respectfully.","Review what the client chooses to own as a next step."],"mnemonic":"FMRC + GSCR."},{"id":"case-15","title":"Retirement and loss of meaning","scenario":"After retiring from a long career, Owen, 46, says, \"Without my work I do not know who I am.\" they has become socially withdrawn, anxious about aging and preoccupied with whether life still has purpose.","question":"Demonstrate an existentially oriented session while also assessing for depression and risk. (25mks)","approach":"Existential therapy with screening for depressive disorder","points":["Use empathic exploration of the transition and identity loss.","Assess depressive symptoms, functioning, medical issues and suicide risk.","Explore meaning previously obtained from work and other possible sources of meaning.","Discuss freedom and responsibility in shaping the next life chapter.","Acknowledge existential anxiety, aging, mortality and isolation without trying to erase them.","Clarify values and chosen commitments.","Identify relationships, contribution, creativity or experiences that can express those values.","Set one concrete action consistent with meaning/connection.","Avoid imposing the therapist's own life philosophy.","Review and follow up on both existential and clinical symptoms."],"mnemonic":"FMRC: Freedom, Meaning, Responsibility, Choice."},{"id":"case-16","title":"Irrational beliefs and self-worth","scenario":"After a relationship breakup, Patricia, 53, repeatedly says, \"I absolutely must be loved by everyone. If someone rejects me, it proves I am worthless and I cannot stand it.\"","question":"Use REBT to formulate and intervene in this case. Demonstrate the ABCDE model. (25mks)","approach":"REBT","points":["A: identify activating event - breakup/rejection.","B: identify rigid beliefs - must be loved, global self-downing, awfulizing, low frustration tolerance.","C: link beliefs to unhealthy emotion/behavior such as depression, panic, pleading or avoidance.","D: dispute logically, empirically and pragmatically - where is the evidence that rejection makes a whole person worthless?","E: develop effective rational beliefs - prefer love/approval but do not need universal approval; rejection is painful but tolerable.","Use rational emotive imagery or behavioral homework.","Distinguish healthy sadness from self-condemnation.","Set a practice task that tests new beliefs.","Monitor change in emotion and behavior.","Maintain collaborative but active style."],"mnemonic":"ABCDE."},{"id":"case-17","title":"Cultural conflict in counseling","scenario":"Quentin, 60, is torn between personal career/relationship goals and strong expectations from family and community. A previous helper told them, \"You need to stop living by old traditions.\" Quentin felt judged and stopped counseling.","question":"Demonstrate a culturally competent counseling session and explain what the previous helper did wrong. (25mks)","approach":"Culturally responsive person-centered counseling","points":["Acknowledge the prior experience of judgment and rebuild safety.","Practice cultural humility; ask rather than assume what family/community values mean to this client.","Explore both individual and relational/community values.","Avoid cultural imposition and ethnocentrism.","Use empathy, reflection and open questions.","Assess the client's own goals rather than imposing individualistic independence.","Explore possible options and consequences in the client's context.","Identify supports and cultural strengths.","Collaboratively set goals consistent with the client's values.","Reflect on therapist bias and seek supervision/consultation if needed."],"mnemonic":"AKSE: Awareness, Knowledge, Skills, Encounters; ask, do not impose."},{"id":"case-18","title":"Trauma-related guilt and cognitive restructuring","scenario":"Rose, 29, survived an accident in which another person was injured. Although investigators found Rose was not at fault, they repeatedly thinks, \"If I had reacted one second faster none of this would have happened. It was my fault.\" Avoidance and nightmares continue.","question":"Demonstrate a CBT-oriented intervention for trauma-related guilt while respecting trauma-treatment pacing. (25mks)","approach":"Trauma-focused CBT principles with cognitive restructuring and graded exposure when ready","points":["Assess PTSD symptoms, dissociation, safety and current functioning.","Identify hindsight bias, inflated responsibility and counterfactual thinking.","Examine evidence for and against personal responsibility using what was knowable at the time.","Develop a balanced statement acknowledging regret without false blame.","Use behavioral reduction of avoidance and trauma-focused exposure only with consent/readiness.","Teach grounding for high arousal/dissociation.","Address meaning and self-forgiveness carefully.","Use homework such as thought records.","Monitor nightmares/avoidance and risk.","Coordinate other care if symptoms are severe."],"mnemonic":"Responsibility check: Control? Knowledge at the time? Intent? Realistic alternatives?"},{"id":"case-19","title":"Perfectionism and overcontrol","scenario":"Samuel, 36, spends so much time checking and perfecting work that deadlines are missed. At home they insists family members follow detailed schedules and becomes upset when others do tasks differently. Samuel sees these standards as necessary and correct.","question":"What personality pattern may be present? Show how you would assess and begin psychotherapy without confusing it with OCD. (25mks)","approach":"OCPD features - CBT/personality-focused assessment","points":["Identify pervasive perfectionism, rigidity and control consistent with OCPD features.","Differentiate from OCD by asking about intrusive obsessions and rituals; OCPD can be ego-syntonic standards rather than unwanted obsessions.","Assess impact across work, relationships and flexibility.","Explore costs and benefits of rules/perfectionism.","Identify all-or-nothing beliefs and fear of mistakes.","Use behavioral experiments with \"good enough\" performance and delegation.","Build cognitive flexibility and collaborative goals.","Address interpersonal impact and empathy.","Avoid power struggles over control.","Review progress using functional outcomes, not just symptom labels."],"mnemonic":"OCPD = Order, Control, Perfectionism, Duty."},{"id":"case-20","title":"Treatment planning after assessment","scenario":"Following assessment, Tina, 43, reports depressed mood, social withdrawal, irregular sleep and work absence. No imminent suicide plan is found, but functioning is poor. Tina says the main wish is \"to get back to a normal routine and feel useful again.\"","question":"Construct a treatment plan showing problem statements, goals, measurable objectives, interventions and review. (25mks)","approach":"Collaborative treatment planning","points":["Define problems in observable, client-centered language (depressed mood, withdrawal, sleep disruption, work impairment).","Write a broad client goal linked to the client's wish.","Create SMART objectives, e.g. two scheduled activities on four days/week; consistent wake time; one supportive contact/week.","Select interventions linked to formulation: behavioral activation, CBT thought work, sleep routine, support building.","Include ongoing risk monitoring.","Specify review date/criteria and responsibility for homework.","Include referral/medical review if indicated.","Document strengths/protective factors.","Ensure plan is collaborative and culturally realistic.","Revise according to measured progress."],"mnemonic":"PGOIR: Problem, Goal, Objectives, Interventions, Review."},{"id":"case-21","title":"Group support after bereavement","scenario":"A counselor is asked to lead a new group for adults who have recently lost spouses. Members differ in culture, age and length of bereavement. Several say they feel isolated because friends avoid talking about grief.","question":"Design and demonstrate the opening of this group, including group type, agreements, counseling skills, cultural issues and group-development considerations. (25mks)","approach":"Support/psychoeducational bereavement group","points":["Clarify purpose and suitability/screening.","Explain informed consent, group confidentiality and its limits.","Establish agreements on respect, attendance, turn-taking and nonjudgment.","Use a forming-stage orientation and invite introductions at a safe pace.","Normalize diversity of grief without prescribing a single timeline.","Use active listening, empathic reflection and universality.","Attend to cultural/religious meanings of mourning.","Prevent monopolizing/scapegoating and monitor group roles.","Include psychoeducation/coping where appropriate without replacing member support.","Close with summary, grounding and next-session expectations."],"mnemonic":"FSNPA stages + AKSE culture + group confidentiality."},{"id":"case-22","title":"Confidentiality and threat to another person","scenario":"During therapy, Victor, 57, describes intense anger toward a named former partner and gives a specific plan to cause serious harm soon. Victor has access to the means described and asks the therapist to promise secrecy.","question":"Explain and demonstrate the therapist's ethical response, including risk assessment, confidentiality limits, documentation and protective action. (25mks)","approach":"Duty to protect/warn scenario","points":["Take the threat seriously and assess specificity, intent, timing, means, access, past violence, substances and protective factors.","Remind the client of previously explained limits of confidentiality.","Do not promise secrecy when serious danger is present.","Consult supervisor/legal/emergency procedures promptly where possible without delaying necessary protection.","Take reasonable protective steps required by applicable law/policy, which may include emergency services and warning/protecting identifiable persons.","Disclose the minimum necessary information.","Continue to engage the client in de-escalation and voluntary safety steps if possible.","Document assessment, consultations, reasoning and actions.","Arrange appropriate emergency/psychiatric evaluation.","Follow up on safety and continuity of care."],"mnemonic":"TIP + MIN: Threat, Identifiable target, Protect; Minimum necessary disclosure."},{"id":"case-23","title":"Child abuse disclosure in a group","scenario":"In an adolescent counseling group, a 14-year-old quietly states that a caregiver has been sexually abusing them. Other members fall silent and the young person asks the counselor not to tell anyone.","question":"Demonstrate the counselor's immediate and follow-up response, including group management, safety and mandated reporting principles. (25mks)","approach":"Safeguarding/mandated reporting","points":["Respond calmly, believe/take the disclosure seriously and thank the young person for telling.","Do not conduct a detailed forensic interrogation in the group.","Protect privacy within the group as much as possible and arrange a private conversation promptly.","Assess immediate safety and urgent medical needs.","Explain that abuse is a limit to confidentiality and that required safeguarding/reporting steps must be taken.","Follow applicable child-protection law/policy and supervisor/agency procedure without unnecessary delay.","Do not confront the alleged abuser personally in a way that could increase danger.","Document the disclosure accurately using the child's own words where possible.","Support the group after the disclosure without revealing unnecessary details.","Arrange trauma-informed follow-up and protective supports."],"mnemonic":"SAFE: Support, Assess immediate danger, Follow reporting law, Ensure follow-up."},{"id":"case-24","title":"Teletherapy privacy and informed consent","scenario":"A therapist plans video sessions with Xavier, who lives in a crowded home and often joins from a shared device. Connection sometimes drops, and the therapist has not discussed what to do in an emergency.","question":"Identify the major ethical issues and show how you would establish a safer teletherapy arrangement. (25mks)","approach":"Technology ethics and informed consent","points":["Discuss privacy at both therapist and client locations.","Use an appropriately secure platform/device and explain relevant limits/risks.","Verify identity and current location at sessions when clinically necessary for emergency response.","Obtain informed consent specific to teletherapy.","Agree on what to do if connection fails.","Create an emergency/contact plan and know local emergency resources where relevant.","Protect records and access credentials.","Clarify whether the client can speak privately and use headphones/alternative location if safe.","Practice only within competence and applicable jurisdictional rules.","Document consent and contingency plans."],"mnemonic":"SPACE: Security, Privacy, Access/identity, Consent, Emergency plan."},{"id":"case-25","title":"Dependence on authority and Adlerian formulation","scenario":"Yvette, 40, says they has always felt \"less capable than everyone else\" and avoids decisions unless a supervisor or parent approves them. they wants to become more independent but expects failure before trying.","question":"Use Adlerian therapy as the anchor theory. Show how you would formulate the lifestyle pattern and carry out an intervention. (25mks)","approach":"Adlerian therapy","points":["Establish an egalitarian, encouraging relationship.","Explore early experiences, family context and the client's private logic/lifestyle conclusions.","Identify inferiority feelings and the goal served by avoiding decisions (e.g., avoiding failure/disapproval).","Emphasize behavior as purposeful rather than simply caused by the past.","Explore social relatedness and belonging.","Use encouragement to separate mistakes from global worth.","Reframe responsibility as the capacity to choose new action.","Set a small \"act as if\" or graded independent decision experiment.","Review consequences and revise mistaken beliefs/goals.","Maintain collaboration rather than therapist authority, so therapy does not reproduce dependency."],"mnemonic":"GSCR: Goal-directed, Social relatedness, Conscious choice, Responsibility."},{"id":"case-26","title":"Early relationships and psychoanalytic formulation","scenario":"Zachary, 47, repeatedly chooses distant partners and becomes intensely angry when the therapist reschedules a session, saying, \"People always leave when I need them.\" Childhood involved repeated separation from a primary caregiver.","question":"Use a psychoanalytic/psychodynamic framework to formulate the case and demonstrate how transference, resistance and interpretation might be handled. (25mks)","approach":"Psychoanalytic/psychodynamic therapy","points":["Develop a therapeutic alliance and invite free exploration of current and early relationships.","Notice the recurring abandonment theme.","Recognize possible transference when the therapist's rescheduling is experienced through earlier expectations of abandonment.","Attend to resistance/avoidance without shaming.","Use clarification before deeper interpretation.","Offer tentative, timed interpretations rather than declaring hidden truth.","Monitor countertransference in the therapist and use supervision.","Connect past pattern, present relationships and here-and-now therapy when the client can use the insight.","Maintain boundaries and consistency.","Help the client develop insight and new relational choices."],"mnemonic":"FIRT: Free association, Interpretation, Resistance, Transference."},{"id":"case-27","title":"Counseling-skills micro-session","scenario":"Chantal, 54, says, \"Everything at home is falling apart. I am angry all the time, but when anyone asks what is wrong I just say I am fine.\" During the session Chantal speaks quickly, looks down and repeatedly clenches both hands.","question":"Demonstrate a counseling micro-session using active listening, paraphrasing, reflection of feeling, open questions, therapeutic confrontation and summarizing. (25mks)","approach":"Core counseling skills","points":["Use attending behavior and silence to communicate presence.","Paraphrase the content without parroting.","Reflect emotion: anger, distress, perhaps fear or helplessness, checking accuracy.","Use open questions such as \"What happens at home when the anger builds?\"","Notice nonverbal cues.","Use gentle confrontation of discrepancy: saying \"fine\" while describing collapse/clenched hands.","Avoid judgment or advice before understanding.","Summarize themes and check accuracy.","Assess safety if statements suggest hopelessness/violence/self-harm.","Collaboratively identify what the client wants from counseling."],"mnemonic":"LEAPS: Listen, Empathize, Ask, Paraphrase, Summarize."}],"exams":[{"set":1,"exam":"exams/Psychotherapy_Practice_Set_01_EXAM.pdf","solutions":"exams/Psychotherapy_Practice_Set_01_SOLUTIONS.pdf"},{"set":2,"exam":"exams/Psychotherapy_Practice_Set_02_EXAM.pdf","solutions":"exams/Psychotherapy_Practice_Set_02_SOLUTIONS.pdf"},{"set":3,"exam":"exams/Psychotherapy_Practice_Set_03_EXAM.pdf","solutions":"exams/Psychotherapy_Practice_Set_03_SOLUTIONS.pdf"},{"set":4,"exam":"exams/Psychotherapy_Practice_Set_04_EXAM.pdf","solutions":"exams/Psychotherapy_Practice_Set_04_SOLUTIONS.pdf"},{"set":5,"exam":"exams/Psychotherapy_Practice_Set_05_EXAM.pdf","solutions":"exams/Psychotherapy_Practice_Set_05_SOLUTIONS.pdf"},{"set":6,"exam":"exams/Psychotherapy_Practice_Set_06_EXAM.pdf","solutions":"exams/Psychotherapy_Practice_Set_06_SOLUTIONS.pdf"},{"set":7,"exam":"exams/Psychotherapy_Practice_Set_07_EXAM.pdf","solutions":"exams/Psychotherapy_Practice_Set_07_SOLUTIONS.pdf"},{"set":8,"exam":"exams/Psychotherapy_Practice_Set_08_EXAM.pdf","solutions":"exams/Psychotherapy_Practice_Set_08_SOLUTIONS.pdf"},{"set":9,"exam":"exams/Psychotherapy_Practice_Set_09_EXAM.pdf","solutions":"exams/Psychotherapy_Practice_Set_09_SOLUTIONS.pdf"},{"set":10,"exam":"exams/Psychotherapy_Practice_Set_10_EXAM.pdf","solutions":"exams/Psychotherapy_Practice_Set_10_SOLUTIONS.pdf"}],"sourceTitles":["Treatment plan formulation consideration","Substance use disorder 1","Assessment and diagnosis 6","Psychosis 1","Ethical and professional issues begins","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; Issues faced by beginning therapists 5","Anxiety disorder 7","Depression 3","More on Informed Consent","Technology and ethical issues","Simulation; THE CASE OF STAN 4","Assessment and diagnosis 5","Continuing of education; Dual relationships","Cultural identity 1","Counseling as a Helping Profession","Informed consent; termination of therapy","Introduction to Psychopathology","Psychoanalytic therapy;  APPLICATION TO THE CASE OF STAN","EXISTENTIAL THERAPY AND TECHNIQUES 3","Depression 9","Assessment and diagnosis 3","EXISTENTIAL THERAPY AND TECHNIQUES 6","Still on Code of ethics","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; Issues faced by beginning therapists 9","Psychoanalytic therapy;  Interpretation; Resistance 1","Psychoanalytic therapy; Ego defense mechanisms","Depression 1","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; Characteristics of effective therapists 2","Assessment and diagnosis 1","Depression 4","Counseling Skills 1","Record keeping for a client","Assessment and Diagnosis","EXISTENTIAL THERAPY AND TECHNIQUES 4","Eating disorders begin","Psychoanalytic therapy;  Application to group counseling ","Depression 2","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; Issues faced by beginning therapists 1","ALFRED ADLER'S THERAPY 4","Reasons why people seek PST 02","Skilled; Unskilled counselors","ALFRED ADLER'S THERAPY 8","Simulation; THE CASE OF STAN 1","Simulation","Ethical principle of nonmaleficience 1","Psychoanalytic therapy; Freud's stages of development","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques 2","Counseling skills 2","Assessment and diagnosis 10","Approaches to PST 3","Steps to effective treatment plan","Legal concerns in counseling; expert witness","Approaches to PST 5","Depression begins","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; Characteristics of effective therapists 1","Ethical principle of Autonomy","Anxiety disorder 3","Simulation; THE CASE OF STAN 5","Brief history of PST","ALFRED ADLER'S THERAPY 3","intro to PST","Ethical principle of Autonomy 1","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques","Depression 6","ALFRED ADLER'S THERAPY 1","Assessment and diagnosis 7","Professional negligence; Malpractice","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; Issues faced by beginning therapists 6","Psychoanalytic therapy; Relationship between the client and the therapist 1","Psychoanalytic therapy; Relationship between the client and the therapist","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; The counselor as a person and a professional","Psychoanalytic therapy; Counter transference","ALFRED ADLER'S THERAPY 2","Ethical principle of Beneficence; Justice","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; Issues faced by beginning therapists 8","Depression 5","Approaches to PST 2","Code of ethics; standards of practice","Assessment and diagnosis 9","Psychoanalytic therapy;  APPLICATION TO THE CASE OF STAN 1","Psychosis Begins","Treatment plan 1","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques 1","Anxiety disorder 4","Approaches to PST","Substance Use disorder begins","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; Issues faced by beginning therapists 2","ALFRED ADLER'S THERAPY","Treatment plan 2","Competence; progress evaluation; professional conduct","Confidentiality; Dual relationships","Psychoanalytic therapy;  Interpretation; Resistance","Psychoanalytic therapy; Functions of the therapist","Things to note in PST","Treatment plan","Psychoanalytic therapy; Superego","Disability concerns; slandering; defamation as ethical concerns","Substance use disorder 2","Anxiety disorder","Counseling skills 5","ALFRED ADLER'S THERAPY 5","Goals of PST","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; Issues faced by beginning therapists 4","Psychoanalytic therapy;  Analysis and interpretation of transference","Psychoanalytic therapy; Characteristics","Counseling skills 4","Counseling Skills","Cultural identity","Counseling skills 6","Cultural identity 2","Counseling relationship; responsibilities as per code of ethics","Counseling relationships continues","Duty to warn","Simulation; THE CASE OF STAN 3","EXISTENTIAL THERAPY AND TECHNIQUES 5","Anxiety disorder 2","Record keeping for a client; case notes","Assessment and diagnosis 8","Counseling skills 3","eating disorders 1","Anxiety disorder 6","EXISTENTIAL THERAPY AND TECHNIQUES ","Approaches to PST 4","Ethical principle of nonmaleficence begins","Simulation; THE CASE OF STAN","EXISTENTIAL THERAPY AND TECHNIQUES 1","Eating disorder 3","Psychosis 2","Anxiety disorder 1","Some reasons why people seek PST","Who seeks PST","Depression 7","Eating disorder 4","Simulation; THE CASE OF STAN 2","Psychoanalytic therapy;  Interpretation technique","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; Characteristics of effective therapists","Hazards of the Helping Profession","Depression 8","Introduction to Psychoanalytic therapy ","EXISTENTIAL THERAPY AND TECHNIQUES 7","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; Issues faced by beginning therapists 3","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; Issues faced by beginning therapists 7","Psychoanalytic therapy;  Application to group counseling 1","Other types of anxiety disorder","ALFRED ADLER'S THERAPY 7","Introduction to counseling theories, methods and techniques; Issues faced by beginning therapists","Assessment and diagnosis 2","What must be considered for pST to work","Eating disorders 2","Psychoanalytic therapy; Role of the therapist","Ethical principle of Veracity","Assessment and diagnosis 4","Case formulation","Anxiety disorder 5","Types of psychotherapy","ALFRED ADLER'S THERAPY 6","EXISTENTIAL THERAPY AND TECHNIQUES 2","ALFRED ADLER'S THERAPY; APPLICATION TO THE CASE OF STAN","ALFRED ADLER'S THERAPY; APPLICATION TO THE CASE OF STAN 1","ALFRED ADLER'S THERAPY; APPLICATION TO THE CASE OF STAN 2"],"categoryNames":{"psychopath":"Psychopathology","techniques":"Theories & Techniques","ethics":"Ethics & Legal","process":"Culture, Groups & Process"},"sourceNote":"This revision site synthesizes the recovered lecture/source map and the supplied 2025 VTI-AEDA examination style. It is not a verbatim transcript or an official answer key. Detailed explanatory notes are exam-oriented teaching syntheses built around the source-supported topics."};
